# honest-check declared in .hd — the static checker, the honesty gate. honest-check checks honest-framework
# APPLICATION code (the languages honest-parse recognizes, e.g. Python and JavaScript), not .hd files and not
# its own internals: check_source parses one application source file, resolves the framework's own
# construction call sites in it, builds a declaration graph, and runs the HC rules over that graph (section
# 3.4: rules operate on the graph, not on raw ASTs); startup_check is the door, and it
# hands a source tree to check_paths, which reads each file with read_source, reads the project's
# honest-check.toml with load_config, and runs the eligible rules at framework startup. honest-check
# takes no language: honest-parse owns the extension-to-grammar table and detects each file's grammar
# from its path, so one run may cover a mixed tree with no language named at the boundary.

module honest-check

  layer correctness

  # The surface, closed: every side of the world a boundary here touches, and no other.
  inputs = { "file" }
  outputs = { "stderr" : "the standard-error stream startup_check prints violations to, in the 'warn' and 'halt' paths of section 2.3",
              "stdout" : "the standard-output stream the CLI door writes the formatted report to, through write_report, in every CLI run (section 2.1, section 6)" }

  type Diagnostic = {
    rule_id: RuleId
    severity: str
    message: str
    source_location: str
    context: str
    fixable: bool
  } note "one finding from one rule against one place in the source. honest-check returns a list of these and never throws for a violation, because a violation is data, not an exception. rule_id is the HC rule that fired, drawn from the RuleId vocabulary — the closed 49-rule catalogue honest-check runs — and written as its catalogue identifier: HC001, HC-P001, HC-REF005, HC-SM01, and so on; the JSON output names this field 'rule'. severity grades the finding and is exactly one of three words. error means the code is dishonest and the finding blocks the gate (exit 1). warning means a smell that is reported without turning the module away. info means either a finding honest-check cannot decide statically and defers to honest-test, or a suppressed or above-adoption-level diagnostic downgraded to info so it stays visible rather than being dropped. message is the human sentence naming what is wrong and, by convention, how to fix it. source_location is where the finding sits, as file:line:col (for example src/chains/report.py:67:1); the JSON output splits it into separate file, line and col fields. context is the offending source snippet quoted verbatim from that location, the exact line (or lines) the finding points at, for example 'if role == \"admin\":'; it gives a reader the code in question without opening the file, and is the empty string when the rule has no single line to quote. fixable is true when the finding sits in the conservative auto-fix subset honest-check can correct mechanically, the same subset the --fix flag applies and the LSP offers as a code action, and false when correcting it needs a human judgement; it lets a tool decide whether to offer an automatic fix."

  # honest-design owns the shape of a parsed .hd module; the declaration-crossing path takes it as
  # foreign rather than redeclaring a shape it does not own, exactly as feature_gen does. read_hd
  # hands back one of these per module; the crossing rules read its functions off it.
  type Declaration = foreign "honest-design" note "one parsed .hd module, the value honest-design's read_hd produces for a single `module` block (read_hd returns {\"ok\": {\"modules\": [Declaration, ...]}}). It is foreign because honest-design, not honest-check, owns the shape; honest-check receives it and reads it, never constructs it. The declaration-crossing rules read these facts off each of its functions: the function name, its role (one of boundary_in, orchestrator, fn, boundary_out — which HC-R002 crosses against the call graph), whether its declaration says awaited (HC-R003), whether its declared return names an absence arm NOTHING or Absent (HC-R004), its declared return in general, and its declared parameter list, whose length HC-REF005 crosses against the code. read_declaration pulls one of these from a named .hd file and hands it to cross_declaration; it is never a .hd declaration honest-check parses itself, since parsing .hd is honest-design's job."

  type RuleConfig = dict<str, dict<str, int>> note "the resolved per-rule settings honest-check runs under (section 3.1), keyed by rule id. The outer map goes from a configurable rule's catalogue id to that rule's settings; the inner map goes from one setting's name to its integer value. honest-check configures only the rules it runs, and it runs exactly one configurable rule: HC-OR003, whose single setting min_run is the minimum run of consecutive equivalent operations two orchestrators must share before HC-OR003 reports suspected duplication (section 3.1, §HC-OR003). HC-P008 (max_lines) and HC-P012 (max_mocks) are honest-test's rules, which honest-check does not run (section 9.4), so they are never keys here. min_run has no built-in default: the documented value 3 is a recommendation, not a value load_config fills in, and HC-OR003 runs only when a project sets min_run in honest-check.toml, so a chosen threshold is never confused with a value nobody set (§HC-OR003). HC-OR003 is therefore the outer map's one possible key and no other rule id is ever a key; HC-OR003's inner map carries min_run as its one setting. Every setting value is an integer — min_run is a count — so the inner value type is int and never an open 'any'. A project sets the value with a per-rule table in honest-check.toml, [rules.HC-OR003] with min_run = N. A resolved RuleConfig is the empty map when honest-check.toml sets nothing, because there is no default to fill in, and {\"HC-OR003\": {\"min_run\": N}} when the project sets min_run = N. There is no default fill-in and no always-present rule id."

  # The rule engine declared as data: the catalogue check_source folds over is RuleId, split into the
  # two disjoint sets that are the module's one behavioural distinction — the rules startup_check runs
  # and the rest. Declared so the engine is named and counted, never buried in a "runs every rule" phrase.
  set startup_rules = {
    "HC001" : "link in a chain has no vocabulary declared",
    "HC002" : "chain type mismatch between adjacent links",
    "HC003" : "recognizer overlap within a vocabulary (error on a decidable Set-versus-Set overlap, info when a predicate is involved)",
    "HC006" : "composed type references an unknown base type",
    "HC007" : "empty chain (a chain() constructed with no links)",
    "HC011" : "catch-all recognizer (accepts nearly all inputs); at startup it fires through actual construction",
    "HC-SM01" : "transition-table state not in the states vocabulary",
    "HC-SM02" : "transition-table event not in the events vocabulary",
    "HC-SM05" : "initial state not in the states vocabulary",
    "HC-SYN" : "source does not parse — gates every other rule; runs first at startup, because a file that does not parse has no architecture to check (section 2.3)"
  } note "the ten startup-eligible HC rules: the parse gate HC-SYN, the construction-time rules, and the two cheapest static rules (HC001, HC002) honest-check runs at framework startup, exactly the ten section 2.3 names. HC-SYN runs first, because a file that does not parse has no architecture to check, so a parse failure blocks startup ahead of every other rule (section 2.3). The full-codebase AST rules are too slow to run as the host application boots, so startup_check runs only these ten while check_source runs the whole RuleId catalogue. Each member but HC-SYN can emit an error-severity diagnostic (section 8); HC003 emits error only on a decidable Set-versus-Set overlap and info when a predicate is involved; HC-SYN emits error when the source does not parse."
  set non_startup_rules = {
    "HC004" : "dead vocabulary type (defined, never bound or composed)",
    "HC005" : "unused binding entry",
    "HC008" : "impure link (framework tier)",
    "HC009" : "predicate may throw on non-matching input",
    "HC010" : "declared emission never produced",
    "HC-SM03" : "unreachable state",
    "HC-SM04" : "dead state (no outgoing transitions)",
    "HC-P001" : "if/elif/else dispatch chain (branches are not data)",
    "HC-P002" : "exception caught in a non-boundary function",
    "HC-P003" : "class declaration (inheritance or bare class)",
    "HC-P004" : "I/O inside a non-boundary function",
    "HC-P005" : "isinstance() or type() in business logic",
    "HC-P006" : "cache without a profiling annotation",
    "HC-P007" : "instance state in a constructor",
    "HC-P010" : "non-serializable return value",
    "HC-P011" : "framework lifecycle hook",
    "HC-P013" : "unbounded database routing key",
    "HC-P014" : "recognizer reused across slots",
    "HC-P016" : "nonlocal closure over mutable state",
    "HC-P017" : "serializer not declared as a chain link",
    "HC-P018" : "unbounded call target (provably-unbounded call site)",
    "HC-R001" : "orphan function (no role, not reachable)",
    "HC-R002" : "output boundary invokes an orchestrator or an input boundary",
    "HC-R003" : "declaration and code disagree about awaiting (async def without `awaited`, or `awaited` over a plain def)",
    "HC-R004" : "declaration and code disagree about absence (code can return the host nothing while the card names no NOTHING/Absent arm, or the reverse)",
    "HC-R005" : "a declared function body yields a stream (a generator) while its declaration returns a value",
    "HC-OR001" : "orchestrator calls another orchestrator",
    "HC-OR003" : "suspected duplication between orchestrators",
    "HC-A001" : "no AuthProvider registered; actor-using operations unverifiable",
    "HC-A002" : "actor trusted from request input instead of the boundary",
    "HC-SUP001" : "suppression matched no diagnostic (dead directive)",
    "HC-SUP002" : "suppression carries no reason",
    "HC-REF001" : "template action target resolves to no mounted route",
    "HC-REF002" : "template include/extends target resolves to no template",
    "HC-REF003" : "template class reference resolves to no defined rule",
    "HC-REF004" : "behavioural attribute value names no declared vocabulary member",
    "HC-REF005" : "declaration and implementation do not resolve to each other",
    "HC-REF006" : "a seam enters M.d but module M declares no function d",
    "HC-REF007" : "a seam enters M.d but d is not a door",
    "HC-REF008" : "a cross-module side effect names a module but no door",
    "HC-REF009" : "two modules meet at a named resource and neither names the other",
    "HC-REF010" : "a world-less module that exactly one module calls",
    "HC-ST001" : "write to persisted state outside an I/O-boundary function",
    "HC-ST002" : "shadow copy of user state outside the manifest-declared DOM",
    "HC-HF001" : "feature_state references a flag not declared in FEATURES",
    "HC-HF002" : "handler table missing an entry for a declared flag state"
  } note "the forty-six HC rules honest-check runs that section 2.3 does not list among the startup-eligible ten: the full-codebase static and reference rules too costly to run as the host boots, together with the two suppression guarantees, all of which run in CLI and LSP mode. check_source runs the single-source members over one file; the five declaration-crossing members (HC-R002, HC-R003, HC-R004, HC-R005, HC-REF005 — the crossing_rules set) need a module's .hd crossed against its code, which check_source does not hold, so cross_declaration runs those on the --declaration path instead (section 2.1). startup_check runs none of these. Five are the cross-module seam and coupling rules section 8 adds (HC-REF006, HC-REF007, HC-REF008 are errors on a seam that fails to resolve to a door; HC-REF009 and HC-REF010 are questions about coupling that report but never fail a run, carrying section 8's Question severity). HC-R002, HC-R003 and HC-REF005 pair their id with the description section 8's Complete Rule Summary gives them; HC-R004 and HC-R005 carry the description section 8's prose gives them (§HC-R004, §HC-R005) and are listed here, though section 8's summary table has not yet been extended to name them, because the crossing path honest-check runs them. Each member carries the severity section 8 states."
  vocabulary RuleId = { startup_rules, non_startup_rules } note "the closed catalogue of HC rule ids honest-check runs, as the union of two disjoint sets — startup_rules (ten, run at startup) and non_startup_rules (forty-six, run only in CLI and LSP mode), 56 members in all. Fifty-four are section 8's Complete Rule Summary minus the three test-time rules honest-test owns and honest-check does not verify: HC-P008 (gherkin step too long), HC-P009 (roled function without a gherkin) and HC-P012 (excessive test doubles), per section 1 and section 9.4; the other two are HC-R004 and HC-R005, which section 8 defines in prose (§HC-R004, §HC-R005) but whose summary table does not yet list, added here because honest-check runs them on the declaration-crossing path. A RuleId value is exactly what a Diagnostic's rule_id carries when that rule fires, and exactly what a --rule flag may name. check_source runs every single-source member of RuleId over one declaration graph; the five declaration-crossing members (crossing_rules) run on cross_declaration's path instead, because they cross a declaration against code and check_source holds no declaration. The exact Diagnostic a given rule produces is pinned per rule by the conformance suite (section 9.2), not by any function declared in this module."

  # The declaration-crossing engine declared as data: the five rules cross_declaration folds over,
  # named so the engine is counted (five), never left in a "runs the selected crossing rules"
  # phrase. Each needs both a module's .hd and its code in view at once, which check_source (one
  # source, no declaration) cannot give, so they run on cross_declaration's path (section 2.1).
  set crossing_rules = {
    "HC-R002" : "a function the .hd marks boundary_out whose code calls a function the .hd marks orchestrator or boundary_in — an output boundary invoking inward (§HC-R002)",
    "HC-R003" : "a declared function whose code async-ness disagrees with its declared awaited: an async def the .hd does not mark awaited, or awaited over a plain def (§HC-R003)",
    "HC-R004" : "a declared function whose code can return the host nothing (an explicit `return None` or a bare `return`) while its declared return names no absence arm (NOTHING or Absent), or a card naming an absence arm over code that never returns nothing (§HC-R004)",
    "HC-R005" : "a declared function whose body yields — a generator wearing a value's card — while its .hd says it returns a value, and which is not a context manager (§HC-R005)",
    "HC-REF005" : "the .hd and the code do not resolve to each other: a function the package exports that the .hd names nowhere, a name the .hd declares that the code defines nowhere, or a declared parameter count that disagrees with the code (§HC-REF005)"
  } note "the five declaration-crossing HC rules: the rules that read a module's .hd against its code, so each needs both artifacts in view at once and none is decidable from a single source. cross_declaration runs the subset of these named in its rule_ids; check_source runs none of them, because it holds one source and no declaration. HC-R002, HC-R003, HC-R004 and HC-R005 read what the .hd says about one function (its role, its awaited flag, its absence arm, whether it returns a value) and cross it against the one fact the code states outright (its call graph, its async keyword, whether it can return None, whether its body yields); HC-REF005 crosses the .hd's declared function set against the code's defined set and the package's exported set. All five are error severity (section 8). HC-R002, HC-R003 and HC-REF005 are also non_startup_rules members (section 8's Complete Rule Summary lists them); HC-R004 and HC-R005 are defined in section 8's prose and added to non_startup_rules so the catalogue honest-check runs names them. The exact source-and-declaration-to-diagnostic behavior of each rule is pinned by the conformance suite (section 9.2), not by any per-rule function declared here, exactly as check_source's single-source rules are."
  vocabulary CrossingRule = { crossing_rules } note "the closed set of five declaration-crossing rule ids cross_declaration runs (section 2.1 --declaration). rule_ids on cross_declaration is a list of these: the gate names them with repeated --rule flags — HC-R002, HC-R003, HC-R004 and HC-R005 together for the role-and-shape crossing, or HC-REF005 on its own alongside --exports for the surface crossing. Every CrossingRule member is also a RuleId member, so a --rule value naming one is a valid RuleId; CrossingRule is the narrower type the crossing core takes, so a reader sees the crossing engine is exactly these five and no other rule can be handed to it."

  # honest-parse detects the grammar from each file's extension; honest-check names no language, so
  # there is no second copy of honest-parse's extension-to-grammar table in this module.
  fn check_source : (context: CheckContext, declaration: Declaration | NOTHING) -> list<Diagnostic> invokes check_construction, check_per_file, apply_suppression note "the pure core of the checker (section 3.4). It takes the already-built CheckContext for one source — the shared parse build_context produced, holding the parsed root, the node list, the import-alias map, the declared role map, the source bytes, the path and the resolved per-rule settings — runs every rule in the RuleId catalogue (both startup_rules and non_startup_rules) over the declaration graph that context carries (vocabularies, bindings, links, chains, state machines — section 3.4), and returns the findings as data. The rules read this graph, not raw ASTs (section 3.4). check_source does not build the context: build_context builds it once and the orchestrator passes it in, so check_source opens no file, parses nothing itself, executes none of the application code it inspects, and modifies nothing. It threads the context through the two rule sub-cores — check_construction (the construction-time rules of section 4.1) and check_per_file (the static per-file rules of section 4.2) — then runs apply_suppression over their combined findings to resolve the inline and file suppression directives; the shared AST helpers the rules call (the nodes, analysis and bindings layer of section 3.4) are reached from inside those two sub-cores, not from here, so check_source invokes only check_construction, check_per_file and apply_suppression. context is the one CheckContext build_context assembled for this source: its source field is the full text of one honest-framework application source file — code written against the framework in a language honest-parse recognizes (Python, JavaScript or TypeScript, Ruby, Go — section 3.2, section 4.1), never a .hd declaration and never honest-check's own internals — whose construction call sites (vocabulary(), binding(), chain(), composed(), state_machine() — section 4.1) build_context already resolved into the declaration graph the rules read; its path field is where that text came from, under whose extension honest-parse selected the grammar, and which stamps each diagnostic's source_location. The HC008 impurity watch lists are keyed by the language honest-parse detected for this file (IO_WATCH_LIST[language], NONDETERMINISTIC_WATCH_LIST[language]), so a mixed tree is checked file by file, each under its own grammar and its own watch lists, with no language named at the boundary. The context also carries the resolved per-rule settings (a RuleConfig load_config produced at the boundary): honest-check's one configurable rule is HC-OR003, whose setting min_run has no built-in default; when the project sets min_run = N the settings map is {\"HC-OR003\": {\"min_run\": N}}, and when it sets nothing the map is empty. A rule absent from the map takes no configuration; HC-OR003 in particular runs only when min_run is set (§HC-OR003), so an empty map means HC-OR003 does not fire. Each rule in the RuleId catalogue has its own source-to-diagnostic behavior — the exact Diagnostic a given rule produces for a given source — and that behavior is pinned by the conformance suite (section 9.2, python/honest-check/conformance/suite.json), which holds one case per rule category giving a source string and its expected diagnostics; the rules are data the check_source core runs, not separately declared functions here, so a specific rule's finding is asserted through that suite, not through a per-rule function-point. declaration is the module's parsed .hd for the file under check when one is available, and the absence value NOTHING when the code has no .hd — an adopter codebase, or a loose snippet — so a run always hands check_source a bounded value here and no downstream path has to tell 'absent' from 'forgot'. It is the same foreign Declaration honest-design's read_hd produces that the crossing rules read, handed to check_source already parsed: the read of the .hd is done at the boundary, not here — run_check finds the nearest enclosing module .hd and reads it through read_declaration — so check_source stays pure and opens no .hd. The three per-file boundary rules consult this declaration only on the path where they would otherwise emit a finding, and only then: HC-P002 (an exception caught in a non-boundary function), HC-P004 (I/O inside a non-boundary function) and HC-P005 (isinstance/type in business logic) each decide a function's boundary-ness from its decorators (@boundary, @link(boundary=True)), and a function that decorator check finds non-boundary — the function the rule is about to flag — is re-checked against the declaration; if the declaration marks that same function boundary_in, boundary_out or reader, the finding is dropped. So a function the framework declares a door in its .hd but leaves undecorated — honest-check's own startup_check and main, which print to stderr and read argv yet carry no decorator — is no longer flagged falsely. When declaration is NOTHING there is nothing to re-check against, so the decorator verdict stands and the finding is emitted as before; the other rules in the catalogue ignore the declaration entirely. It returns the list of every Diagnostic the rules produced over this one source; an empty list means the source is clean. It raises nothing: a violation comes back as a Diagnostic, never as an exception."

  reader fn read_source : (path: str) -> bytes side_effect reads "file" note "the reader on the filesystem side. It pulls one file's complete contents from disk and hands them back as raw bytes, so the orchestrator can pass those bytes to build_context. It returns the file's bytes, not a decoded string, because honest-parse parses bytes and reports byte offsets: a node's span is read by slicing those same bytes, and a decoded string would misalign the moment the file held a non-ASCII character (section 3.4). It reads and does nothing else: it does not decode, parse, check, or write. path is the filesystem path of the one file to read, and it is always a concrete source file: file discovery at the boundary (section 3.2) walks each requested path, appends only files whose extension honest-parse recognizes, and drops anything an exclude glob matches, so a directory, a path that does not exist, and a file of an unrecognized extension are filtered out before check_paths runs and never reach read_source. It returns that file's full contents as bytes. The spec states no fault for a path that becomes unreadable after discovery — a permission error, or a file deleted between the walk and the read — so read_source declares none and names no absence case here. The spec gives honest-check a single catch-all for a run that cannot complete, exit code 2 (configuration error or internal failure, section 2.1), but does not state that an unreadable file routes there, so whether such a path raises, and what it raises, the spec leaves undecided. Its one side effect is reading the filesystem."

  reader fn load_config : () -> RuleConfig side_effect reads "file" note "the config reader on the filesystem side (section 3.1). It finds honest-check.toml in the nearest ancestor directory of the working directory, reads it, and resolves the project's declared per-rule settings into the rule_config map the orchestrator passes to check_source. It reads the filesystem and resolves what it read: it does not parse source, check, or write. It takes no argument because the startup door has no --config flag (section 2.3) and the config location is the nearest ancestor of where the host application runs, which is filesystem state the boundary reads directly. It returns a map from each configurable rule's id to that rule's settings. honest-check configures only the rules it runs, and it runs exactly one configurable rule: HC-OR003, whose single setting min_run has no built-in default (section 3.1, §HC-OR003). HC-P008 (max_lines) and HC-P012 (max_mocks) are honest-test's rules, configured in honest-test's own config and never here (section 9.4), so no other rule id ever appears. A project sets min_run with a per-rule table in honest-check.toml: under the [rules] section a header [rules.HC-OR003] introduces min_run = N. load_config returns {\"HC-OR003\": {\"min_run\": N}} when the project sets min_run = N, and the empty map when honest-check.toml sets nothing or no honest-check.toml exists — min_run has no documented default to fill in, so a project that sets nothing leaves HC-OR003 unconfigured, and HC-OR003 runs only when min_run is set (§HC-OR003). The resolution happens at the boundary (section 3.1, §HC-OR003): load_config returns what the project declared and check_source takes the result as a required argument, so a run configured with nothing is a run where HC-OR003 does not fire and check_source never has to invent a threshold. Its one side effect is reading the filesystem."

  orchestrator fn check_paths : (paths: list<str>) -> list<Diagnostic> invokes read_source, load_config, build_context, check_source note "the orchestrator the door hands its paths to. It pulls the project's resolved rule_config with load_config at the start of the run and applies that one map to every file: for each path it was given, it pulls that file's text with read_source, builds that file's shared parse with build_context from the text, the path and the one rule_config, and runs the pure core check_source over that context (with the absence value NOTHING for the declaration, since startup crosses no .hd), folding every file's diagnostics into one combined list. The orchestrator does no reading or parsing itself: read_source reads, build_context parses, and check_source runs the rules (section 3.4). One resolved configuration governs the whole run, so no file is checked under different settings and the result does not depend on how many files there are; resolving the config a single time rather than once per file is an efficiency of the orchestrator, not an observable part of the contract. honest-check names no language: each file's grammar is detected by honest-parse from the file's extension inside check_source, so a mixed tree checks file by file with no language threaded through. paths is the list of filesystem paths to check, each already resolved to a concrete source file: file discovery (walking a directory, keeping only files whose extension honest-parse recognizes, and dropping anything matched by an exclude glob, section 3.2) happens at the boundary before this orchestrator runs. It returns one flat list of every Diagnostic found across all the files, in the order the paths were given. An empty list means every file was clean."

  boundary_in fn startup_check : (paths: list<str>, on_error: str, severity: str) -> list<Diagnostic> raises HonestCheckError invokes check_paths side_effect writes "stderr" note "the door the framework's development-mode startup integration calls (section 2.3). The host application calls it with the source paths; it hands them to check_paths, gets back the folded diagnostics, decides whether any finding reaches the blocking severity, acts per on_error, and returns the same diagnostic list check_paths produced. honest-check names no language at this door: each file's grammar is detected by honest-parse from the file's extension (section 3.2), so a mixed tree is covered by one call and the door carries only paths, on_error and severity, matching the section 2.3 startup example exactly. At startup it runs only the startup_rules set — the startup-eligible subset of the RuleId catalogue, the parse gate HC-SYN plus the construction-time and light static rules HC001, HC002, HC003, HC006, HC007, HC011, HC-SM01, HC-SM02, HC-SM05 (section 2.3) — because the full-codebase AST rules (non_startup_rules) are too slow for startup. HC-SYN runs first: a file that does not parse has no architecture to check, so a parse failure blocks startup ahead of every other rule (section 2.3), and it emits error when the source does not parse. Each of the other nine can emit a diagnostic at error severity (section 8): HC001, HC002, HC006, HC007, HC-SM01, HC-SM02 and HC-SM05 are error-only; HC003 emits error on a decidable Set-versus-Set recognizer overlap (and info when a predicate is involved); and HC011, which at startup fires through actual construction (section 4.1), rejects a catch-all recognizer at error severity. An error-severity finding is exactly what the documented severity 'error' counts as a block. For a concrete trigger, a framework chain() call constructed with no links fires HC007 at error severity — in the application code honest-check reads, not a .hd declaration and not a parse failure: section 4.1's check_HC007 fires on IF len(chain.links) == 0, and in CLI and startup mode honest-check reaches that verdict by analyzing the chain() call site. So an application-code chain() built with an empty link list produces one blocking diagnostic at startup; under on_error 'halt' and severity 'error' that prints the violation to stderr and exits the process with code 1. paths is the list of source paths to check, for example ['src/'], handed straight to check_paths. on_error says how a block behaves and is exactly one of three words: 'warn' prints the blocking violations to stderr and lets startup continue; 'raise' raises HonestCheckError carrying the full report, stopping startup by exception; 'halt' prints the blocking violations to stderr and then exits the process with code 1, which stops startup the way a failed precondition stops a program. severity is the minimum severity a diagnostic must reach to count as a block, one of 'error', 'warning' or 'info'; the documented setting is 'error', meaning only error-severity diagnostics block startup while warnings and infos are reported but never block. A diagnostic below this threshold never triggers on_error. Its one side effect is writing to stderr: both 'warn' and 'halt' print the blocking violations there, and 'raise' writes nothing because it carries the report in the exception instead. The process exit is part of the 'halt' path only: after printing, it exits the process with code 1, as section 2.3 states, and startup_check does not return in that case. It raises HonestCheckError only when on_error is 'raise' and at least one diagnostic reaches severity; the error carries the full report."

  # startup_check is the door the world reaches. It hands its paths to one orchestrator (check_paths),
  # which pulls each file and runs the pure core (check_source) over it. A call-form entry, so the
  # entry-check reports it UNCHECKED rather than confirming it.
  entry "call:startup" -> startup_check

  # ======================================================================================
  # The command-line interface (section 2.1). The primary invocation surface: a door reads
  # the argument vector, one orchestrator discovers the source files, pulls the config, runs
  # the same pure core (check_source) over each file, prepares the report, renders it in the
  # chosen format, writes it to stdout, and returns the process exit code. The pure core both
  # the startup orchestrator (check_paths) and this orchestrator (run_check) share is
  # check_source itself; the per-file read-and-check loop is each orchestrator's own pull, not
  # a second copy of the checking logic. run_check runs in one of two modes, chosen by whether
  # --declaration names a module's .hd: the base mode just described, which runs check_source over
  # each discovered file; and the declaration-crossing mode (section 2.1 --declaration / --exports),
  # which reads that one .hd and runs cross_declaration once over the whole package of discovered
  # files to cross the declaration against the code, running only the declaration-crossing rules
  # (crossing_rules).
  # Both modes share the same discovery, report, format, write and exit-code path.
  # ======================================================================================

  # ---- the bounded words of the CLI surface ----

  # The four output formats section 6 defines. --format selects one; the default is human.
  set formats = {
    "human"  : "the human-readable CLI default (section 6.1): the adoption line, then one block per finding — 'file:line:col: severity rule_id', the message and a reference line, and the quoted source lines — closing with a 'Found N errors, M warnings.' tally",
    "json"   : "the machine format for CI and tooling (section 6.2): an object with version, timestamp, a summary of error/warning/info counts carrying adoption and enforced_rules, and a diagnostics array whose entries split source_location into file, line and col and carry rule, severity, message, context and fixable",
    "github" : "GitHub Actions workflow-command annotations (section 6.3): one '::error' or '::warning' line per finding with file, line, col and title=<rule id>, so findings surface inline on a pull request",
    "junit"  : "JUnit XML (section 6.4): a <testsuites name=\"honest-check\"> document with one <testsuite> per file and a <testcase>/<failure> per finding, for CI systems that consume JUnit"
  } note "the exact four --format words section 6 defines. parse_args resolves an absent or unrecognized --format to 'human', so the by_format dispatch only ever receives a declared member and 'human' is the no-match fallback."
  vocabulary Format = { formats } note "the closed set of output-format words --format accepts (section 2.1); the default, and the no-match fallback the dispatch falls back to, is 'human' (section 6.1)."

  set severities = {
    "error"   : "the code is dishonest; the finding blocks the gate and, at or below the declared adoption level, drives exit code 1 (section 2.1, section 8)",
    "warning" : "a smell reported without turning the module away (section 8)",
    "info"    : "a finding honest-check defers to honest-test, or a suppressed or above-adoption-level diagnostic downgraded so it still reports but never blocks (section 8, section 9.4)"
  } note "the three severity words (section 8). --severity names the minimum a finding must reach to appear in the output (section 2.1); the default is 'warning', so info findings are hidden unless --severity info is passed. This is the same three-word grading the frozen Diagnostic.severity field carries, declared here as a vocabulary for the --severity threshold the CLI applies."
  vocabulary Severity = { severities } note "the closed severity grading the --severity reporting threshold uses (section 2.1); the default is 'warning'."

  set adoption_levels = {
    "Structural" : "the shape of the code holds: no classes, no dispatch chains, no hidden state in closures or constructors, no lifecycle hooks (section 9.4)",
    "Boundary"   : "Structural plus: I/O, faults, persistence and identity cross only at declared boundaries; the interior is pure (section 9.4)",
    "Typed"      : "Boundary plus: the vocabulary, chain and state-machine type system holds (section 9.4)",
    "Declared"   : "Typed plus: everything is declared rather than inferred — roles, orchestration, static references and user state (section 9.4); the strictest level, and the level an absent declaration means"
  } note "the four cumulative adoption levels of section 9.4. A codebase declares one in honest-check.toml [check] adoption; an absent declaration means 'Declared', the strictest, because silence never buys leniency. Each level enforces its own rules and every rule of the levels below it."
  vocabulary AdoptionLevel = { adoption_levels } note "the closed set of adoption-level names a codebase may declare (section 9.4), carried through to the adoption line every run prints."

  # The section 9.4 level-to-rule membership, declared as data so enforced_rules_of folds a named
  # set, never a 'rules above the level' phrase. Four cumulative level sets plus the always-on three.
  set structural_rules = { "HC-P001", "HC-P003", "HC-P007", "HC-P011", "HC-P016" } note "the five rules the Structural level adds (section 9.4): no dispatch chain, no class, no constructor state, no lifecycle hook, no mutable-closure state."
  set boundary_rules = { "HC-P002", "HC-P004", "HC-P005", "HC-P006", "HC-P010", "HC-P013", "HC-ST001", "HC-A001", "HC-A002" } note "the nine rules the Boundary level adds (section 9.4): exception caught in the interior, I/O in the interior, isinstance/type in business logic, unprofiled cache, non-serializable return, unbounded routing key, state write outside an I/O boundary, and the two identity rules."
  set typed_rules = { "HC001", "HC002", "HC003", "HC004", "HC005", "HC006", "HC007", "HC008", "HC009", "HC010", "HC011", "HC-SM01", "HC-SM02", "HC-SM03", "HC-SM04", "HC-SM05", "HC-P014", "HC-P017" } note "the eighteen rules the Typed level adds (section 9.4): the vocabulary and chain rules HC001-HC011, the state-machine rules HC-SM01-HC-SM05, HC-P014 (recognizer reused across slots) and HC-P017 (serializer not declared as a chain link)."
  set declared_rules = { "HC-R001", "HC-R002", "HC-R003", "HC-R004", "HC-R005", "HC-OR001", "HC-OR003", "HC-REF001", "HC-REF002", "HC-REF003", "HC-REF004", "HC-REF005", "HC-REF006", "HC-REF007", "HC-REF008", "HC-ST002", "HC-HF001", "HC-HF002" } note "the eighteen rules the Declared level adds (section 9.4): the role rules HC-R001-HC-R005 (including the three declaration-crossing rules HC-R004, HC-R005 and HC-REF005, which section 9.4 now places at Declared so they fail the build rather than downgrading to info), the orchestration rules HC-OR001 and HC-OR003, the static-reference rules HC-REF001-HC-REF008, shadow user state HC-ST002, and the two feature-flag rules."
  set always_rules = { "HC-SYN", "HC-SUP001", "HC-SUP002" } note "the three rules that hold at every level (section 9.4): the parse gate HC-SYN and the two suppression guarantees. A codebase that does not parse has no level, and the suppression guarantee of section 7.4 cannot be level-dependent."
  vocabulary AdoptionRule = { structural_rules, boundary_rules, typed_rules, declared_rules, always_rules } note "every HC rule that belongs to some adoption level: the union of the four cumulative level sets and the always-on three, 53 members in all. Its membership is exactly the section 9.4 table, and enforced_rules_of selects the enforced subset by level. The six rules belonging to no level — HC-P008, HC-P009, HC-P012 (honest-test's, which honest-check does not run) and HC-REF009, HC-REF010, HC-P018 (questions or unassigned) — are deliberately absent, which is why the adoption denominator is 53, not the 59 of the full catalogue (section 9.4)."

  set exit_codes = {
    "0" : "no errors: the run found nothing at a blocking severity (warnings and infos may be present), or the run was a --report run, which always exits 0 (section 2.1, section 2.1.1)",
    "1" : "one or more errors at or below the declared adoption level: the gate fails (section 2.1)",
    "2" : "a configuration error or an honest-check internal failure: the run could not complete (section 2.1)"
  } note "the three process exit codes section 2.1 defines. resolve_exit_code decides 0 versus 1 from the prepared report; 2 is the catch-all the door exits with when the run cannot complete."
  vocabulary ExitCode = { exit_codes } note "the closed set of process exit codes the CLI door returns to the shell (section 2.1)."

  # ---- the CLI data dictionary ----

  # The read failure read_declaration can answer with: a configured .hd that is missing or does not
  # parse (§HC-R002: "a gate that reports clean over a declaration it could not open is no gate").
  # It travels by return — read_declaration -> run_check -> the door — and the door discharges it as
  # exit code 2, the run-could-not-complete code (section 2.1).
  type Fault = {
    problem: str
    remedy: str
  } note "the one failure honest-check's own functions answer with as a value, raised by nothing in the interior: the declaration-read failure read_declaration returns when the configured .hd is missing or does not parse (§HC-R002, exit code 2). It is honest-check's own Fault record, not honest-design's — honest-design owns the shape of a parsed Declaration, but the failure to read one is honest-check's to report. problem states what went wrong in a sentence a programmer reads, for example that the .hd path named by --declaration does not exist or that honest-design could not parse it. remedy states what to do instead, for example to correct the --declaration path or to fix the declaration so honest-design can parse it. The two fields together make the fault self-explaining, so a reader never has to open honest-check to know what the run could not do and how to fix it. A Fault travels by return: read_declaration answers Declaration | Fault, run_check routes the Fault outward in its ExitCode | Fault answer, and the door (main) discharges it by stopping the world at exit code 2, because a gate that returned a clean code over a declaration it could not open would be no gate."

  type CliArgs = {
    paths: list<str>
    format: Format
    severity: Severity
    include_rules: list<RuleId>
    exclude_rules: list<RuleId>
    config_path: str
    report_mode: bool
    declaration: str
    exports: str
  } note "the resolved command-line arguments parse_args produces from the raw argv (section 2.1). paths are the positional source folders and files to check, each a filesystem path exactly as typed (for example 'src/'); discover_files later walks them. format is the resolved --format, a Format value, defaulted to 'human'. severity is the resolved --severity reporting threshold, a Severity value, defaulted to 'warning': a finding below it is not shown. include_rules is the list of rule ids named by --rule (repeatable); when non-empty the run reports only those rules, and empty means every rule runs. In declaration-crossing mode the gate names the crossing rules here (--rule HC-R002 --rule HC-R003 --rule HC-R004 --rule HC-R005, or --rule HC-REF005), so include_rules carries the CrossingRule ids cross_declaration is asked to run. exclude_rules is the list of rule ids named by --no-rule (repeatable), suppressed from the output. Both lists hold RuleId catalogue ids. config_path is the --config value, the explicit path to honest-check.toml, or the empty string when --config was not given and the nearest-ancestor honest-check.toml is used (section 3.1). report_mode is true when --report was given: count every rule across the tree and exit 0 (section 2.1.1). declaration is the --declaration value: the filesystem path to the one module .hd to cross against the discovered code, or the empty string when --declaration was not given. A non-empty declaration selects the crossing mode, in which run_check reads that .hd and runs cross_declaration once over the whole package of discovered files instead of check_source per file (§HC-R002: the rule runs only where a declaration is named, never guessed by walking up from a source file). exports is the --exports value: the filesystem path to the package's export module — in Python its src/<pkg>/__init__.py, the file carrying __all__ — which HC-REF005 needs to read the exported surface, or the empty string when --exports was not given. exports is set only alongside --declaration and --rule HC-REF005; the other four crossing rules do not read it. --fix and --watch (section 2.1) are out of this layer and parse_args does not record them."

  type ProjectConfig = {
    exclude: list<str>
    adoption: AdoptionLevel
    format_manifest: str
  } note "the honest-check.toml [check] settings this layer needs, read by load_project_config (section 3.1). exclude is the list of glob patterns [check] exclude declares, which discover_files drops matches of; it is the empty list when none is set. adoption is the [check] adoption value, the AdoptionLevel the codebase declares, resolved to 'Declared' (the strictest) when the key is absent (section 9.4). format_manifest is the [check] format_manifest value, the filesystem path to honest-format's emitted manifest.json that HC-REF004 resolves hf-* attribute values against; it is the empty string when the key is unset, and an empty string stands HC-REF004 down, so the rule runs only where an application declares which honest-format vocabulary its templates target (§HC-REF004). This is a different slice of the same honest-check.toml than load_config's RuleConfig: load_config returns only the [rules] per-rule integer settings, never the [check] section, so the [check] section is read here."

  type RuleCount = dict<str, int> note "per-rule finding counts across the whole tree for --report (section 2.1.1): the outer key is a rule id as its catalogue string (a RuleId value), and the value is how many findings that rule produced over every discovered file. prepare_report fills it only for a --report run, and it is the empty map otherwise; --report prints one row per rule from it, marking which rules the declared adoption level enforces."

  type CheckReport = {
    diagnostics: list<Diagnostic>
    errors: int
    warnings: int
    infos: int
    rule_counts: RuleCount
    adoption: AdoptionLevel
    enforced_count: int
    total_count: int
    report_mode: bool
  } note "the fully prepared result the formatters render and resolve_exit_code judges (section 6, section 2.1). diagnostics is the surviving findings after the adoption downgrade, the --severity threshold and the --rule/--no-rule filters have been applied, in the order the files were discovered. errors, warnings and infos are the counts of those surviving findings by severity, the tally the human format's 'Found N errors, M warnings.' line and the JSON summary report. rule_counts carries the per-rule counts for a --report run, and is the empty map otherwise. adoption is the declared AdoptionLevel; enforced_count is how many rules that level enforces (the size of enforced_rules_of(adoption)); total_count is 53, the rules the adoption levels cover (section 9.4); together they render the adoption line every run prints: 'adoption level <adoption> — <enforced_count> of <total_count> rules enforced.' report_mode is carried from the arguments so resolve_exit_code can force exit 0 and the formatters can render the per-rule table."

  # ---- the format dispatch (section 6): the format word picks the renderer ----
  dispatch by_format = { "human" -> format_human, "json" -> format_json, "github" -> format_github, "junit" -> format_junit } note "picks the output formatter by the resolved --format word (section 6). The key space is the Format vocabulary and parse_args resolves an absent or unrecognized --format to 'human' before dispatch, so the table is total over what it can receive and 'human' is its no-match fallback; there is no wildcard row because the key is bounded to a declared Format member before the dispatch runs, the same way honest-type's by_kind is bounded to its recognizer kinds."

  # ---- the pure core of the CLI path ----

  fn parse_args : (argv: list<str>) -> CliArgs note "turns the raw argument vector into the resolved CliArgs (section 2.1). It reads the positional paths and the base-mode flags: --config (the path to honest-check.toml, else nearest-ancestor), --format (human|json|github|junit, resolved to 'human' when absent or unrecognized so the dispatch is total), --severity (error|warning|info, the minimum a finding must reach to be reported, resolved to 'warning' when absent), --rule (restrict the run to the named rule ids, repeatable, collected into include_rules), --no-rule (suppress the named rule ids, repeatable, collected into exclude_rules), --report (set report_mode true: count every rule across the tree and exit 0, section 2.1.1), --declaration (the path to the one module .hd to cross against the code, collected into declaration), and --exports (the path to the package's export module that HC-REF005 reads, collected into exports). Absence is resolved here, at the boundary, to an explicit bounded value — an omitted --format becomes 'human', an omitted --severity becomes 'warning', an omitted --declaration or --exports becomes the empty string — so no downstream function has to tell 'chose' from 'forgot', and an empty declaration is exactly the signal run_check reads to stay in base mode. argv is the full argument vector the door read from the process, already in memory; parse_args opens no file (read_declaration, read_source, load_config and load_project_config do the filesystem reads) and performs no check. It ignores --fix and --watch, which this layer does not carry. It returns the CliArgs the orchestrator fans out to discover_files, read_declaration, cross_declaration, prepare_report and the dispatch."

  fn enforced_rules_of : (level: AdoptionLevel) -> list<AdoptionRule> note "returns every HC rule enforced at the given adoption level — the section 9.4 cumulative membership. Because the levels are cumulative, the result always includes always_rules (HC-SYN, HC-SUP001, HC-SUP002, which hold everywhere); Structural adds structural_rules; Boundary adds boundary_rules on top; Typed adds typed_rules on top of that; and Declared adds declared_rules, returning the whole AdoptionRule set — all 53 placed rules, so a codebase that declares nothing is held at Declared and its adoption line reads '53 of 53'. Its length is the enforced_count the adoption line prints, and a rule NOT in the result is one prepare_report downgrades to info because the declared level does not enforce it (section 9.4). level is the AdoptionLevel the codebase declared; it reads nothing and returns pure data."

  fn prepare_report : (diagnostics: list<Diagnostic>, args: CliArgs, adoption: AdoptionLevel) -> CheckReport invokes enforced_rules_of note "turns the raw per-file findings into the report the formatters render and resolve_exit_code judges (section 2.1, section 6, section 9.4). It applies, in order: the adoption level — every diagnostic whose rule is not in enforced_rules_of(adoption) is downgraded to info, so an above-level finding still reports but never blocks (section 9.4); then the --severity threshold in args — a finding below the threshold is dropped from the output; then the --rule/--no-rule filters in args — keep only args.include_rules when that list is non-empty, and drop args.exclude_rules. It then counts the surviving findings into errors, warnings and infos; fills rule_counts with each rule's finding count when args.report_mode is set (section 2.1.1) and leaves it empty otherwise; and records adoption, enforced_count (the size of enforced_rules_of(adoption)) and total_count (53, the rules the levels cover, section 9.4) so the adoption line can be rendered. report_mode is carried from args. Suppression directives are resolved earlier, inside check_source, so prepare_report does not re-resolve them. diagnostics is the concatenation of every discovered file's check_source findings; args is the resolved CliArgs; adoption is the declared level from the project config. It reads nothing and returns the CheckReport."

  fn format_human : (report: CheckReport) -> str note "renders the human-readable CLI default (section 6.1). It opens with the adoption line 'honest-check: adoption level <report.adoption> — <report.enforced_count> of <report.total_count> rules enforced.' (section 9.4), then one block per diagnostic: a 'file:line:col: severity rule_id' header split from the diagnostic's source_location, the message and any reference line, and the quoted context lines with their line numbers, blocks separated by a blank line; it closes with the 'Found N errors, M warnings.' tally from report.errors and report.warnings. When report.report_mode is set it instead renders the section 2.1.1 per-rule table from report.rule_counts — one row per rule with its severity, finding count, and whether the declared level enforces it. report is the prepared CheckReport. It returns the finished text and performs no I/O; write_report prints it."

  fn format_json : (report: CheckReport) -> str note "renders the JSON machine format for CI and tooling (section 6.2). It produces an object with 'version', a 'timestamp', a 'summary' carrying errors, warnings and infos (and, per section 9.4, 'adoption' and 'enforced_rules'), and a 'diagnostics' array whose entries split each diagnostic's source_location into separate 'file', 'line' and 'col' fields and carry 'rule' (the rule_id), 'severity', 'message', 'context' and 'fixable'. When report.report_mode is set it carries the per-rule counts from report.rule_counts instead of (or alongside) the findings. report is the prepared CheckReport. It returns the JSON text as a string and performs no I/O."

  fn format_github : (report: CheckReport) -> str note "renders GitHub Actions workflow-command annotations (section 6.3): one line per diagnostic, '::error' for an error-severity finding and '::warning' otherwise, each carrying file, line and col from the diagnostic's source_location, title=<rule id>, and the message as the line body, so findings surface inline on a pull request. The adoption line is emitted as a leading annotation or notice so every run still states its level (section 9.4). report is the prepared CheckReport. It returns the annotation text and performs no I/O."

  fn format_junit : (report: CheckReport) -> str note "renders JUnit XML for CI systems that consume it (section 6.4): a <testsuites name=\"honest-check\"> document with one <testsuite> per file carrying that file's failure count, and within each a <testcase name=\"<rule id>:<line>\" classname=\"<file>\"> holding a <failure> whose message is the finding and whose body quotes the offending source line. report is the prepared CheckReport. It returns the XML text and performs no I/O."

  fn resolve_exit_code : (report: CheckReport) -> ExitCode note "decides the process exit code from the prepared report (section 2.1). When report.report_mode is true the code is '0' always: --report reports, it does not judge, which is what keeps it from being a second quieter gate (section 2.1.1). Otherwise the code is '1' when report.errors is greater than zero — one or more findings at error severity survived the adoption downgrade, which means they are at or below the declared level — and '0' when no error survived, because warnings and infos never block. It never returns '2': exit code 2 is reserved for a configuration error or an internal failure the run could not complete, which the door applies. One such failure is now modelled: read_declaration answers a Fault when the configured .hd is missing or does not parse, run_check routes that Fault outward, and the door (main) discharges it at exit code 2 (§HC-R002) — so the exit-2 path has a producer at last, at the door rather than here. resolve_exit_code still decides only '0' versus '1', from the prepared report, because the report path has no such read failure to detect; the declaration-read Fault never reaches it, having been routed out ahead of prepare_report. report is the prepared CheckReport."

  # ---- the declaration-crossing core (section 2.1 --declaration) ----

  # One source file of the package under the crossing view: its path and its already-read text.
  # cross_declaration takes the whole package as a list of these, because HC-REF005 needs the
  # package's entire defined-function set in view at once (§HC-REF005), which a per-file core cannot
  # supply; run_check pairs each discovered path with its read_source text into one of these.
  type SourceFile = {
    path: str
    text: bytes
  } note "one source file of the package being crossed, as the path it came from and its full contents, paired so cross_declaration sees the whole package at once. path is the filesystem path the text was read from — a concrete, honest-parse-recognized source file that discover_files already walked to and that read_source already opened — used to detect the grammar honest-parse parses this file under and to stamp each diagnostic's source_location; cross_declaration does not re-open it. text is that file's complete contents as raw bytes, not a decoded string, because honest-parse parses bytes and reports byte offsets and a decoded string would misalign on any non-ASCII character (section 3.4): the full byte contents of one honest-framework application source file, code in a language honest-parse recognizes (Python, JavaScript or TypeScript, Ruby, Go — section 3.2, section 4.1), never a .hd declaration and never honest-check's own internals, the same bytes read_source answered and the same artifact check_source takes as its source. A list of these is the whole-package view the crossing rules fold over: HC-REF005 unions every file's defined-function set across the list (§HC-REF005 takes the package's full source_functions), and the four per-function crossing rules (HC-R002, HC-R003, HC-R004, HC-R005) read each file in turn."

  type FormatVocabulary = dict<str, list<str>> note "honest-format's emitted manifest as HC-REF004 reads it (§HC-REF004): each enumerated hf-* attribute name mapped to the closed list of values that attribute accepts — hf-format to the format names, hf-type to the input-type names, each hf-*-format option to its enumerated set. read_format_manifest loads it from the manifest.json path [check] format_manifest names, never by scraping the client runtime's dispatch tables (declared, never inferred). An attribute carrying a free value — hf-decimals, hf-currency, a raw prefix — names no enumerated vocabulary and is simply absent from the map, so HC-REF004 does not judge it; only the attributes the map carries are resolved. The NOTHING absence arm, not an empty map, is what stands HC-REF004 down where no manifest is configured, so 'no manifest' is told from 'a manifest that enumerates nothing'."

  fn pair_source : (path: str, text: bytes) -> SourceFile note "pairs one discovered path with its already-read bytes into a SourceFile, the one new pure function the per-package restructuring needs. run_check calls it once per discovered file in crossing mode, after discover_files gave it the path and read_source gave it the bytes, to assemble the list<SourceFile> whole-package view cross_declaration takes. It exists because the declaration's producer model credits only a reader's answer or a function's return as the producer of a declared type: read_source answers a file's bytes and discover_files answers a path (a str), but neither answers a SourceFile, so the record that joins a path to its bytes has to be returned by some function for the crossing core to be fed it. path is the filesystem path discover_files walked to; text is that file's full byte contents read_source pulled — raw bytes, not a decoded string, so honest-parse parses them and reports byte offsets without the non-ASCII misalignment a decoded string would carry (section 3.4). It reads nothing, opens nothing and decides nothing — it only joins the two values into the record — and returns the SourceFile carrying them."

  fn cross_declaration : (sources: list<SourceFile>, declaration: Declaration, export_source: bytes, rule_ids: list<CrossingRule>) -> list<Diagnostic> note "the pure core of the declaration-crossing path, the crossing-mode counterpart of check_source. It crosses one module's .hd declaration against the module's whole package of source files at once and returns the declaration-crossing findings as data, running only the crossing rules named in rule_ids. It takes the package as a list of SourceFile rather than a single source and path because HC-REF005 crosses the declaration's declared function set against the package's entire defined-function set, which needs every file's definitions in view together (§HC-REF005's algorithm takes the package's full source_functions); a per-file core modelled on check_source could not supply that whole-package set. Like check_source it opens no file, executes no application code, and modifies nothing: read_declaration and read_source do the reads, and it is handed the already-loaded declaration and the already-read source texts. For each SourceFile it parses the text under the grammar honest-parse detects from that file's path extension, reads from that parse each fact the code states outright — each function's name, whether it is `async def`, whether it can return the host nothing (an explicit `return None` or a bare `return`), whether its body yields, and its call graph — and crosses those against what the declaration says about the same-named function; and it unions every file's defined-function set across the whole list for the surface crossing. sources is the whole-package view: a list of SourceFile, each the full text of one honest-framework application source file paired with its path, never a .hd. declaration is the one parsed .hd module (a Declaration) read_declaration loaded from the --declaration path, carrying every function's declared name, role, awaited flag, return type and parameter list. export_source is the full byte contents of the package's export module — in Python its __init__.py, the file carrying __all__ — the bytes read_source answered, never a decoded string, so honest-parse parses them and reports byte offsets without the non-ASCII misalignment a decoded string would carry (section 3.4); HC-REF005 parses them to read the exported surface. It is the empty bytes when --exports was not given, and the four non-REF005 crossing rules ignore it. rule_ids is the list of crossing rules to run, drawn from --rule (CliArgs.include_rules narrowed to CrossingRule members); an empty list runs all five crossing rules. The per-rule crossing logic is nested here as data, exactly as check_source holds the construction rules, and is not separately declared: HC-R002 emits an error when a function the declaration marks boundary_out calls, in the code, a function the declaration marks orchestrator or boundary_in (a callee the declaration does not name is skipped, §HC-R002); HC-R003 emits an error when a declared function's `async def`-ness disagrees with its declared awaited (§HC-R003); HC-R004 emits an error when a declared function can return the host nothing while its card names no NOTHING/Absent arm, or names an absence arm while the code never returns nothing (§HC-R004); HC-R005 emits an error when a declared function's body yields while its card returns a value and it is not a context manager (§HC-R005); HC-REF005 emits an error for a function the package exports that the declaration names nowhere, a name the declaration declares that the package defines nowhere (crossing the declared set against the union of every SourceFile's definitions), or a declared parameter count that disagrees with the code, and emits an error when export_source carries no literal __all__ to read (§HC-REF005). The exact diagnostic each rule produces for a given package and declaration is pinned by the conformance suite (section 9.2), not by any per-rule function here. It returns the list of every Diagnostic the selected crossing rules produced across the whole package; an empty list means the declaration and the package agree. It raises nothing: a disagreement comes back as a Diagnostic, never as an exception."

  # ---- the cross-file reference core (section HC-REF002, HC-REF003) ----

  # The base-mode counterpart of cross_declaration: a pure core that takes the whole discovered set of
  # template, stylesheet and script files at once (as SourceFile records, the same type cross_declaration
  # takes), because an include target may resolve to a sibling root and a class may be defined by any
  # component's stylesheet, so neither reference is decidable from one file. run_check pairs each discovered
  # file into a SourceFile and runs this once over the whole set, after its per-file check_source loop.
  fn check_references : (sources: list<SourceFile>, format_vocabulary: FormatVocabulary | NOTHING) -> list<Diagnostic> note "the pure cross-file reference core of the base-mode CLI path, the whole-set counterpart of check_source for references that resolve across files rather than within one. It takes the whole discovered set of the project's template, stylesheet and script files, already read and paired into SourceFile records, parses each under the grammar honest-parse detects from its path extension (honest-parse carries the html, jinja, css and javascript grammars — section 3.2), builds from the whole set at once the two resolvable sets the reference rules need, resolves each template's include/extends targets and class references against those sets, and returns the findings as data. It is the cross-file counterpart of cross_declaration's whole-package view: a template's `{% include %}` may resolve to a file in a sibling atoms/ or molecules/ root, and a class a template emits may be defined by any component's stylesheet, so no per-file core could resolve either — the resolution needs every template, stylesheet and script in view together. Like check_source and cross_declaration it opens no file (read_source does that), parses no .hd (honest-design's job), executes none of the code it inspects, modifies nothing, and raises nothing: a dead reference comes back as a Diagnostic, never as an exception. sources is that whole-set view: a list of SourceFile, each one discovered template, stylesheet or script file paired with the filesystem path it was read from, the path used to detect the grammar honest-parse parses the file under and to stamp each diagnostic's source_location; check_references does not re-open it. Off each file it reads what that file states: from a template (an .html file, read under honest-parse's Jinja grammar — the HTML grammar alone reads `{% %}` as opaque text, §HC-REF002) it reads each include/extends statement's literal or dynamic target and each static or interpolated class value; from a stylesheet (a .css file) it reads every class selector honest-parse's CSS grammar surfaces (a class_name under a class_selector, so a pseudo-class like :hover is not mistaken for a class); from a script (a .js module) it reads the string-literal arguments to a classList method (add, remove, toggle, replace or contains) and the tokens of a className assignment. HC-REF002 resolves each template's literal `{% include %}` / `{% extends %}` target against the set of templates the whole set holds — a target resolves when it matches a template file relative to any search root, the way the engine's loader searches — and emits an error for a literal target that matches no template; a dynamic target (a variable or expression carrying no string literal, `{% include some_var %}`) has nothing to resolve and is skipped (§HC-REF002). HC-REF003 resolves each static CSS class a template emits, and each string-literal class a script adds through a classList method or a className assignment, against the union of the class selectors every discovered stylesheet defines, and emits an error for a class that no stylesheet defines; a class value carrying interpolation (`class=\"card {{ variant }}\"`) and a non-literal classList/className argument are dynamic and skipped whole, the stated bound of the check and not an oversight (§HC-REF003). Both rules are error severity (section 8). The per-rule crossing logic is nested here as data, exactly as check_source holds its construction rules: HC-REF002 and HC-REF003 are not separately declared functions, and the exact Diagnostic each produces for a given set of files is pinned by the conformance suite (section 9.2, python/honest-check/conformance/suite.json), not by any per-rule function declared in this module. It returns the list of every Diagnostic the reference rules produced across the whole set; an empty list means every reference resolved. It raises nothing. It also holds HC-REF001 and HC-REF004, nested as data exactly as HC-REF002 and HC-REF003 are, so the whole-set view carries two more cross-artifact reference resolutions and sources widens to carry the discovered code files alongside the templates, stylesheets and scripts — the code files the template-reading rules ignore, but HC-REF001 reads. HC-REF001 resolves each template action site — an hx-get/hx-post/hx-put/hx-delete/hx-patch attribute, whose verb names the method and whose value names the path — against the route map, the union of the (method, path) pairs every route_table() function in the code files declares as data (honest-page §9 writes the route table as module-level data a tool reads by parsing, never by running the application), and emits an error for a site that targets a (method, path) no route mounts: a dead action that renders, passes every shape test, and does nothing. A path parameter matches through normalize_path, so an interpolated target (/items/{{item.id}}) resolves against its route pattern (/items/{id}); a fully dynamic value carrying no literal path (hx-get=\"{{ url }}\") is unresolvable and skipped, the same bound HC-REF002 takes for a variable include target. HC-REF001 runs only where the code declares a route table at all: with no route_table() in view the route map is absent and the rule stands down rather than flag every action site, exactly as HC-REF004 stands down with no manifest. format_vocabulary is honest-format's manifest as read_format_manifest loaded it (a FormatVocabulary), or the NOTHING absence arm when [check] format_manifest named no manifest; HC-REF004 resolves each authored hf-* value whose attribute the vocabulary enumerates against that attribute's allowed set, and emits an error for a value naming no member — a dead behavioural reference, the span renders raw and silently formats nothing. An hf-* attribute the vocabulary does not carry is a free-value attribute and is not judged; an interpolated value (hf-format=\"{{ kind }}\") is skipped whole; and the NOTHING arm stands HC-REF004 down entirely, so it runs only where an application declares which honest-format vocabulary its templates target. All four rules are error severity (section 8); the exact Diagnostic each produces is pinned by the conformance suite and the check_references probe (section 9.2), not by any per-rule function declared in this module."

  # ---- the whole-program role core (section HC-R001) ----

  # The whole-program counterpart of check_source for the orphan-function rule HC-R001. The rule is
  # decidable only across a whole package, because a function orphaned in one file may be reached by a
  # call from another, so it cannot live in the per-file check_source. run_check groups the discovered
  # code files by their nearest module .hd and runs this once per group, exactly as check_references
  # runs once over the template/stylesheet/script set and cross_declaration runs once over the crossing
  # package.
  fn check_roles : (sources: list<SourceFile>, declaration: Declaration | NOTHING) -> list<Diagnostic> note "the pure whole-program core of the orphan-function rule HC-R001 (section HC-R001), the whole-package counterpart of check_source for the one role rule no single file can decide. It takes the package's code files — the SourceFile records of the languages honest-parse recognizes as source (Python, JavaScript or TypeScript, Ruby, Go — section 3.2, section 4.1), not templates or stylesheets — and the package's parsed .hd module, or the absence value NOTHING when the code has no module .hd (an adopter codebase, a loose snippet). HC-R001 is a whole-program rule and not a per-file one because a function orphaned in one file may be reached by a call from another, so the reachability question is answerable only with the whole package in view at once, which check_source (one source, no cross-file call graph) cannot supply — the same reason check_references takes the whole template/stylesheet/script set and cross_declaration takes the whole crossing package. A top-level function is ROLED when it carries a role decorator — @link, @recognizer, @boundary, @helper or @orchestrator — or when the declaration names it, because every function a .hd declares carries a role there, so a declared function is roled by its declaration even when its code wears no decorator. It parses each SourceFile under the grammar honest-parse detects from that file's path extension, reads each file's top-level function definitions and their call sites, builds the whole-program call graph across all the sources (which function calls which, by name), computes the set of functions reachable from the roled set through that graph, and emits one HC-R001 error for each top-level function that is neither roled nor reachable — an orphan, which auto-generation cannot reach, so no test is generated for it and the code is dishonest (section HC-R001). When there are NO roled functions at all — no role decorator on any function and no declaration (declaration is NOTHING) — the role system is not in use for this package, and it emits nothing: it never floods a codebase that has not adopted roles with a finding on every function. This is why the framework's own code is clean when its declaration is supplied — every declared function is roled, so the roled set seeds the reachable set and covers the package — and would emit nothing were the declaration absent, because the code carries no role decorators, which makes the roled set empty and stands the rule down rather than flagging every function. sources is the package's whole-package code view: a list of SourceFile, each one code file paired with the path it was read from, the path used to detect the grammar honest-parse parses the file under and to stamp each diagnostic's source_location; check_roles does not re-open it. declaration is the module's parsed .hd (a Declaration, honest-design's IR that read_declaration loaded) when the package has one, and NOTHING when it has none, so a run always hands a bounded value here and the rule never has to tell 'absent' from 'forgot'; it reads the declared function set off the Declaration to seed the roled set, and never constructs it. It opens no file (read_source does that), parses no .hd (honest-design's job), executes none of the code it inspects, modifies nothing, and raises nothing: an orphan comes back as a Diagnostic, never as an exception. The per-rule logic nests here as data, exactly as check_source holds its construction rules and cross_declaration holds the crossing rules; HC-R001 is not a separately declared function, and the exact Diagnostic it produces for a given package and declaration is pinned by the conformance suite (section 9.2), not by any per-rule function here. It returns the list of every HC-R001 error the package produced, all at error severity (section 8); an empty list means every top-level function is roled or reachable, or the package has adopted no roles at all."

  # ---- the whole-program auth core (section HC-A001, HC-A002) ----

  # The whole-program counterpart of check_source for the two identity rules HC-A001 and HC-A002.
  # Provider registration is project-wide — a provider registered in any one file governs every
  # authorizing link across the project — so neither rule is decidable from a single file, and the
  # pass reads across all the discovered code files at once as a single set, exactly as check_roles
  # and check_references read their whole sets. It takes no declaration: it reads provider
  # registration and authorizing links off the code itself, so there is no .hd to cross.
  fn check_auth : (sources: list<SourceFile>) -> list<Diagnostic> note "the pure whole-program core of the two identity rules HC-A001 and HC-A002 (section HC-A001, HC-A002), the whole-package counterpart of check_source for the two rules that turn on project-wide authentication-provider registration. It takes the project's code files — the SourceFile records of the languages honest-parse recognizes as source (Python, JavaScript or TypeScript, Ruby, Go — section 3.2, section 4.1), not templates or stylesheets — and parses each under the grammar honest-parse detects from that file's path extension. Both rules are whole-program and not per-file because provider registration is project-wide: a provider registered in any one file governs every authorizing link across the whole project, so the register-is-present question is answerable only with the whole set in view at once, which check_source (one source) cannot supply — the same reason check_roles takes the whole package and check_references takes the whole template/stylesheet/script set. A provider IS REGISTERED when some file in the set calls honest-auth's register_auth_provider(...), the provider-registration call that installs the boundary validator which resolves an actor. An AUTHORIZING LINK is a top-level function decorated @link(authorizes=True): a link that acts on behalf of an actor, whose actor the framework resolves at the boundary and passes inward as the name actor. The set HC-A001's spec algorithm folds over, 'operations that act on behalf of an actor', is read here as exactly the authorizing links, because that is the operation the framework resolves an actor for; the spec's phrase is broader than this definition, which is honest-check's narrowing of it. HC-A001 (warning): when NO file in the set registers a provider AND the set holds at least one authorizing link, it emits one HC-A001 warning — those links act on behalf of an actor but no boundary validator resolves one, so the identity they act under is unverifiable; the finding names the authorizing links and says to register a provider or remove the actor dependency. It is a warning because an application with no authentication may be intentional (a read-only public application, local development), so the gap is surfaced without blocking the gate (section HC-A001). HC-A002 (error): when a provider IS registered, it checks each authorizing link's body, and for each authorizing link whose body does not reference the name actor it emits one HC-A002 error at that link's location — a link declared authorizes=True that does not use the boundary-resolved actor is sourcing identity from request input (body, query string, form fields, the manifest), which is forgeable; identity is established once at the boundary and must flow inward as the data named actor (section HC-A002). The provider-registered gate is why both rules live in one whole-program pass rather than two: HC-A001 covers the no-provider case and HC-A002 covers the with-provider case, so the two are mutually exclusive over one set and no authorizing link is ever reported by both — a link is flagged by HC-A001 only when no provider is registered anywhere, and by HC-A002 only when one is. sources is the project's whole-package code view: a list of SourceFile, each one code file paired with the path it was read from, the path used to detect the grammar honest-parse parses the file under and to stamp each diagnostic's source_location; check_auth does not re-open it. It opens no file (read_source does that), parses no .hd, executes none of the code it inspects, modifies nothing, and raises nothing: a finding comes back as a Diagnostic, never as an exception. The per-rule logic nests here as data, exactly as check_source holds its construction rules and check_roles holds HC-R001; HC-A001 and HC-A002 are not separately declared functions, and the exact Diagnostic each produces for a given set of files is pinned by the conformance suite (section 9.2), not by any per-rule function here. It returns the list of every HC-A001 and HC-A002 finding the set produced — HC-A001 at warning severity, HC-A002 at error severity (section 8); an empty list means either a provider is registered and every authorizing link references actor, or no provider is registered and the set holds no authorizing link."

  # ---- the CLI boundaries ----

  reader fn discover_files : (paths: list<str>, exclude: list<str>) -> list<str> side_effect reads "file" note "the file-discovery reader (section 3.2). For each requested path: if the path is a file and no exclude glob matches it, keep it; if the path is a directory, recursively walk it and keep every file whose extension honest-parse recognizes and that no exclude glob matches. It returns the flat list of concrete source files to check. paths is the positional paths from the command line (CliArgs.paths); exclude is the glob patterns from honest-check.toml [check] exclude (ProjectConfig.exclude). honest-check names no language: it does not carry its own extension table but asks honest-parse whether each file's extension is recognized (.py, .js, .ts, .mjs, .cjs, .rb, .go — section 3.2), so a mixed tree is discovered in one walk. A directory, a nonexistent path, and a file of an unrecognized extension are filtered out here, so every path that reaches read_source is a concrete, recognized source file. Its one side effect is reading the filesystem."

  reader fn load_project_config : () -> ProjectConfig side_effect reads "file" note "the [check]-section config reader (section 3.1). It finds honest-check.toml in the nearest ancestor directory of the working directory and returns the [check] settings this layer needs: the exclude globs discover_files drops matches of, and the adoption level section 9.4 grades against. It is distinct from load_config, which reads the same file but returns only the [rules] per-rule RuleConfig; the frozen RuleConfig carries only per-rule integer settings and never the [check] section, so [check] is read here. It takes no argument: like load_config it resolves the nearest-ancestor honest-check.toml, and honoring --config's explicit path is not wired through either reader in this layer. exclude is the empty list when [check] exclude is unset; adoption is 'Declared' when [check] adoption is absent (section 9.4); format_manifest is the empty string when [check] format_manifest is unset, which stands HC-REF004 down (§HC-REF004). Its one side effect is reading the filesystem."

  boundary_out fn write_report : (text: str) -> bool side_effect writes "stdout" note "the stdout writer, the CLI path's one output boundary. It prints the already-rendered report text to standard output and returns whether the write completed. text is the finished report a format function produced (section 6); write_report neither formats nor decides anything, it only writes. It is a terminus: it calls nothing inward. Its one side effect is writing stdout."

  reader fn read_declaration : (hd_path: str) -> Declaration | Fault side_effect reads "file" note "the declaration reader on the filesystem side, the crossing path's one new boundary (section 2.1 --declaration). It reads the named .hd file's text from disk and parses it through honest-design's read_hd, returning the one parsed module (a Declaration) that cross_declaration crosses against the code. It reads and parses and does nothing else: it does not check, does not touch the source tree, and does not construct the declaration shape, which is honest-design's to own. hd_path is the filesystem path of the module .hd named by --declaration, the value run_check reads from CliArgs.declaration and calls this only when that value is non-empty; an empty declaration keeps run_check in base mode and this reader is never called. It returns the parsed module on success, and a Fault when the .hd is missing or honest-design cannot parse it — the configured read failure §HC-R002 routes to exit code 2, because a gate that reports clean over a declaration it could not open is no gate. The Fault is a value, not an exception: it travels by return, so run_check carries it outward (ExitCode | Fault) and the door discharges it as exit code 2, the run-could-not-complete code. This is a stricter posture than read_source takes for a source file that becomes unreadable after discovery, and deliberately so: the declaration is the gate's own ground truth, and a run that cannot read it must fail, never pass quietly. Its one side effect is reading the filesystem."

  reader fn read_format_manifest : (path: str) -> FormatVocabulary | NOTHING side_effect reads "file" note "the format-manifest reader HC-REF004 resolves hf-* attribute values against (§HC-REF004). path is the [check] format_manifest value run_check read from ProjectConfig; this reader is called only when that path is non-empty, and returns the NOTHING absence arm for an empty path, so an application that configures no manifest stands HC-REF004 down rather than resolving its hf-* values against an empty vocabulary. For a configured path it reads honest-format's emitted manifest.json from disk and returns the FormatVocabulary it carries — each enumerated hf-* attribute name mapped to its closed list of values — so check_references can resolve each authored value against the declared set. It reads and does nothing else: it does not scan templates, does not scrape the client runtime's dispatch tables (the manifest is declared by honest-format, never inferred), and does not check. The spec states no fault for a configured path that cannot be read or does not parse as the manifest shape; read_format_manifest names no fault here and answers NOTHING, standing the rule down the same way an unset path does, the parallel of read_source naming no fault for a file unreadable after discovery. Its one side effect is reading the filesystem."

  # ---- the CLI orchestrator ----

  orchestrator fn run_check : (argv: list<str>) -> ExitCode | Fault invokes parse_args, load_project_config, discover_files, read_declaration, read_format_manifest, load_config, read_source, pair_source, build_context, check_source, check_references, check_roles, check_auth, cross_declaration, prepare_report, by_format, write_report, resolve_exit_code note "the orchestrator the CLI door hands the argument vector to (section 2.1). It parses the arguments with parse_args; pulls the [check] settings with load_project_config, resolved once for the whole run; and discovers the concrete source files with discover_files over the parsed paths and the config's exclude globs. It then runs in one of two modes, chosen by whether CliArgs.declaration is empty. In base mode (empty declaration) it pulls the per-rule settings with load_config, and for each discovered file pulls its text with read_source, builds that file's shared parse with build_context from the text, the path and the one resolved RuleConfig, and runs the pure core check_source over that context, folding every file's diagnostics into one list — the same per-file read-build-check the startup orchestrator check_paths does, where read_source reads, build_context parses, and check_source runs the rules (section 3.4). For each discovered file it also finds the nearest enclosing module .hd — the honest-*/honest-*.hd of the package the file lives in — and, when one exists, reads and parses it through read_declaration (the same reader the crossing path pulls, so base mode reusing it adds no new invoke) and passes the matching parsed Declaration to check_source as its declaration argument, so the boundary rules HC-P002, HC-P004 and HC-P005 can drop a finding on a function the .hd marks a boundary that carries no decorator; when the file lies under no module .hd — an adopter codebase, a loose snippet — it passes the absence value NOTHING, and the boundary rules keep their decorator verdict. After that per-file loop, still in base mode, it also runs the cross-file reference pass: it pairs each discovered template, stylesheet and script file's path with its read_source text through pair_source into a SourceFile, collects them into one list<SourceFile>, and runs the pure core check_references once over that whole set, folding its HC-REF002 and HC-REF003 diagnostics into the same combined list the per-file check_source findings went into. This pass is separate from the per-file loop because its two rules resolve references that cross files — an include target may live in a sibling atoms/ or molecules/ root, a class may be defined by any component's stylesheet — so check_references needs every template, stylesheet and script in view at once, which a per-file check_source cannot supply. Still in base mode, it then runs the whole-program role pass for HC-R001: it groups the discovered code files — the languages honest-parse recognizes as source, not the templates and stylesheets the reference pass took — by their nearest enclosing module .hd, reusing the same nearest-.hd discovery it already does per file to supply check_source's declaration argument; it pairs each code file's path with its read_source text through pair_source into a SourceFile, and for each group runs the pure core check_roles once over that group's list<SourceFile> with the group's parsed Declaration (the NOTHING absence value for the group of files that lie under no module .hd), folding its HC-R001 diagnostics into the same combined list the per-file check_source and the reference findings went into. This pass is separate from the per-file loop and from the reference pass because HC-R001 is a whole-program rule — a function orphaned in one file may be reached by a call from another — so check_roles needs a whole module's code in view at once, grouped by the .hd whose declared functions seed the roled set, which a per-file check_source cannot supply. Still in base mode, after the role pass, it runs the whole-program auth pass for HC-A001 and HC-A002: it collects the discovered code files — the same languages honest-parse recognizes as source that the role pass grouped — each paired through pair_source into a SourceFile, and runs the pure core check_auth once over that one whole list<SourceFile>, folding its HC-A001 and HC-A002 diagnostics into the same combined list the per-file check_source, the reference pass and the role pass fed. This pass runs over the whole discovered code as a single set rather than grouped by .hd — unlike the role pass — because provider registration is project-wide: a provider registered in any one file governs every authorizing link in the project, so the auth rules need the entire project's code in view at once, and a per-.hd grouping would split a provider from the links it governs. In declaration-crossing mode (non-empty declaration) it pulls the one parsed module with read_declaration over CliArgs.declaration; when that read fails — a .hd that is missing or does not parse — read_declaration answers a Fault, and run_check does not check anything over a declaration it could not open: it routes that Fault straight outward as its own answer (ExitCode | Fault), and the door discharges it as exit code 2 (§HC-R002). With a declaration in hand it pulls the export module's text with read_source over CliArgs.exports when that is non-empty, pulls each discovered file's text with read_source, and pairs each file's path with its text through pair_source into one SourceFile, collecting the whole package as a single list<SourceFile>. It then runs the pure core cross_declaration once over that whole-package batch — crossing the one declaration against every file together, running only the crossing rules named in CliArgs.include_rules — so HC-REF005 sees the package's entire defined-function set at once, and takes back the one combined diagnostic list rather than folding a per-file core. The two modes share the pure core each needs rather than a second copy of any checking logic, and both feed one combined list into the same tail: it prepares the findings with prepare_report (applying the adoption downgrade, the --severity threshold and the --rule/--no-rule filters, and computing the counts and the adoption-line numbers), selects the renderer with the by_format dispatch on the resolved format and renders the report to text, writes that text to stdout through write_report, and returns the exit code resolve_exit_code decides. honest-check names no language anywhere on this path: each file's grammar is detected by honest-parse from its extension inside check_source and cross_declaration. Two details ride on this reference pass for HC-REF001 and HC-REF004. The pass hands check_references every discovered file paired into a SourceFile, not only the templates, stylesheets and scripts — the code files too, because HC-REF001 reads the route map off the route_table() data a code file declares, and the template-reading rules ignore the code files anyway. And before the pass, when ProjectConfig.format_manifest is non-empty, run_check pulls honest-format's manifest with read_format_manifest and hands the FormatVocabulary to check_references for HC-REF004 to resolve hf-* values against; when the path is empty it hands the NOTHING absence arm, standing HC-REF004 down. argv is the raw argument vector from the door; the returned value is an ExitCode on every completed run, or the declaration-read Fault routed outward, and the door turns either into the process exit."

  # ---- the CLI door ----

  boundary_in fn main : (argv: list<str>) -> ExitCode raises HonestCheckError invokes run_check note "the command-line door (section 2.1), the primary invocation surface. The host starts it as 'python -m honest_check.cli <paths...>' with the section 2.1 flags; the pre-commit gate calls it exactly this way and reads its exit code. It carries only I/O: it receives the argument vector, hands it straight to run_check, and turns run_check's answer into the process exit — it holds no parsing, discovery or checking logic, all of which live on run_check's path. run_check answers either an ExitCode or the declaration-read Fault it routed outward. On an ExitCode the door exits the process with it, the same way the startup door's 'halt' path exits the process (section 2.3): 0 means the shell sees success (no errors, though warnings may have printed), and 1 means the gate failed with one or more errors at or below the declared adoption level. On the Fault — a configured .hd that was missing or did not parse (§HC-R002) — the door discharges it at exit code 2, the run-could-not-complete code: it does not return a clean code over a declaration it could not open, because a gate that did would be no gate, so it surfaces the read failure as exit 2, raising HonestCheckError carrying the Fault's problem and remedy rather than letting a returned value be ignored. This is the one place a Fault crosses from a value into a world effect, which is why only this boundary may discharge it. argv is the argument vector the module's __main__ shim read from the process and passed in; that shim is the entry below, reported UNCHECKED. It raises HonestCheckError only on the declaration-read failure run_check routes to it."

  # main is the CLI door the world reaches via `python -m honest_check.cli`. It hands argv to one
  # orchestrator (run_check), which discovers the files, pulls the config, runs the pure core over
  # each file, renders the report, writes stdout, and returns the exit code. A cli:__main__ entry,
  # so the entry-check reports it UNCHECKED rather than confirming it.
  chain cli = main -> run_check
  entry "cli:__main__" -> main

  # ======================================================================================
  # The cross-module seam checks (section 8, §HC-REF006 and §HC-REF007). The honest-pre-commit dogfood gate
  # is the outside world here, and the world enters honest-check through a door, never by calling
  # the pure rule functions straight. So the gate reaches ONE boundary_in, check_seams, handing it
  # every loaded declaration and the in-scope module names; that door hands its inputs to one
  # orchestrator, run_seam_rules, which runs the two cross-module rules (check_hc_ref006 and
  # check_hc_ref007) over the modules in view and folds every finding into one list. The three rule
  # functions below — declared_seams, check_hc_ref006, check_hc_ref007 — are reached only through
  # that orchestrator now (declared_seams by the two REF rules, the two REF rules by run_seam_rules),
  # never by the world directly, exactly as check_source is reached through run_check. They are
  # cross-module rather than single-module: each reads one module's parsed declaration and the set
  # of all loaded declarations, and resolves the seams the one module wrote into the others. They are
  # pure — the gate supplies the declarations already parsed, so they open no file, parse nothing,
  # and touch no world — and they return Diagnostics as data, exactly as check_source and
  # cross_declaration do. HC-REF008, HC-REF009 and HC-REF010 are NOT declared here: HC-REF008 waits
  # on honest-design to mark a bare module name (no dot) as a seam (§649), and HC-REF009/HC-REF010
  # are Question-severity coupling rules blocked on honest-design's world-side vocabulary and caller
  # detection.
  # ======================================================================================

  # The three side-effect verbs honest-design parses, bounded. A seam carries exactly one of these
  # as the way its caller touches the module it enters, so the Seam record names the closed set
  # rather than a loose str.
  set directions = {
    "reads"        : "the caller reads the target",
    "writes"       : "the caller writes the target",
    "reads_writes" : "the caller both reads and writes the target"
  } note "the three side-effect directions the Honest Framework grammar defines (reads, writes, reads_writes). honest-design parses one of these as each side effect's direction; a Seam carries it verbatim, and HC-REF006 and HC-REF007 render it into their message so a reader sees how the caller reaches the door it named."
  vocabulary Direction = { directions } note "the closed set of side-effect directions a seam may carry: reads, writes or reads_writes. It is exactly honest-design's SideEffect.direction value, named here as a vocabulary rather than a plain str because the set of ways one function may touch another is closed."

  # One cross-module reach a module declares: a single side effect of one of the module's functions
  # whose target names another module. The record that declared_seams produces and the two REF rules
  # fold over.
  type Seam = {
    caller: str
    module: str
    door: str
    direction: Direction
  } note "one cross-module reach one module declares — a single side effect, of one of the module's functions, whose target names another module. A declaration never crosses a module boundary: the caller names the callee's door on its own side effect, with a dot — `reads \"honest-parse.parse_source\"` — and the callee never names its callers, which is what lets a library be used by code it has never heard of. So a seam is written once, by the side that knows, and resolving it needs both the caller's declaration and the callee's in view at the same time; `validate` sees one module and reports nothing here, which is why this is honest-check's, like every other reference that resolves or does not. caller is the name of the function that declared the side effect, the side that wrote the seam. module is the named module the seam enters, the module half honest-design split from the dotted target. door is the function name inside that module the seam targets, the door half of the dotted target; it is the empty string when the side effect named a module and no door (a bare `writes \"honest-persist\"`), a seam that carries no door name for HC-REF006 or HC-REF007 to resolve and that HC-REF008 — honest-design's half not yet built, so not declared here — is the rule that will catch. direction is how the caller touches the target, one of reads, writes or reads_writes. honest-check reads this record off a Declaration's functions; it never constructs a declaration, which is honest-design's to own."

  fn declared_seams : (module: Declaration) -> list<Seam> note "collects every seam one module declares into a list of Seam records, the one derivation HC-REF006 and HC-REF007 share rather than each re-deriving it. For each function in module.functions and each side effect that function declares, a side effect whose module half is the empty string names the world — `reads \"HTTP\"`, `writes \"DOM\"`, `reads \"env:POOL_SIZE\"` — and carries no module to resolve, so it is skipped; a side effect whose module half is non-empty is a cross-module seam and becomes one Seam carrying the function's name as the caller, the named module, the door (the side effect's door half, which may be the empty string when the target named a module and no door), and the direction (the side effect's reads/writes/reads_writes). module is the one parsed .hd module whose seams to collect: a Declaration, honest-design's IR for one `module` block, read_hd's value for a single module. It reads nothing, opens nothing, and decides nothing beyond this selection — it only reads each function's side effects off the declaration and keeps the ones that name a module — and returns the list of seams, empty when the module declares no cross-module side effect. check_hc_ref006 and check_hc_ref007 each invoke it, so the set of seams a module declares is derived once and named in one place."

  fn check_hc_ref006 : (module: Declaration, declarations: dict<str, Declaration>, path: str) -> list<Diagnostic> invokes declared_seams note "runs HC-REF006 over the seams one module declares: for each seam into a module that IS present in declarations but that declares no function of the seam's door name, it emits an HC-REF006 error. The caller says it enters `M.d`, the declaration of M names no function d, and the reference is dead — the same fault as a template naming a route nothing mounts: the artifact reads as working and the thing it points at is absent. It invokes declared_seams to get the module's seams, then for each seam applies two skips before resolving: a seam whose door is the empty string names a module and no door and carries nothing to resolve, so it is skipped (that bare-module case is HC-REF008's, not this rule's); and a seam whose named module is not a key in declarations is skipped, not assumed absent — a workspace holds the modules it holds, a declaration may name one from outside it, and reporting a dead reference because the file was not loaded would make the rule depend on how it was invoked, and a gate whose answer changes with its arguments is not a gate (§639). For a seam whose named module is in declarations, if that module's functions include no function named the seam's door, it emits one HC-REF006 error naming the caller, the module and the door. module is the one declaration whose seams are resolved (a Declaration); declarations is every loaded declaration keyed by its module name (the gate supplies them, and the rule judges only those); path is the filesystem path of module's .hd, stamped into each diagnostic's source_location so a reader finds the offending side effect. HC-REF006 and HC-REF007 are separate codes because they call for different repairs: HC-REF006's finding is a wrong name, and someone corrects it. It reads nothing and returns one Diagnostic per dead seam at error severity (section 8); the empty list means every door name a seam of this module named resolved to a function in the named module. It raises nothing."

  fn check_hc_ref007 : (module: Declaration, declarations: dict<str, Declaration>, path: str) -> list<Diagnostic> invokes declared_seams note "runs HC-REF007 over the seams one module declares: for each seam whose door name IS declared in the named module but whose role is not boundary_in, it emits an HC-REF007 error. The name is there and is not a door: the declaration of M does have a function d, and d is not a `boundary_in`, so the world is reaching that module somewhere other than its entrance. A pure function is the common case and the worst one, because it means the core is being used directly. It invokes declared_seams to get the module's seams, then applies the same two skips HC-REF006 does — a seam whose door is the empty string is skipped (HC-REF008's case), and a seam whose named module is not a key in declarations is skipped, not assumed absent, because a gate whose answer changes with how it was invoked is not a gate (§639). For a seam whose named module is in declarations AND declares a function of the seam's door name, if that function's role is not boundary_in, it emits one HC-REF007 error naming the caller, the module, the door and the role the module gave that door. module is the one declaration whose seams are resolved (a Declaration); declarations is every loaded declaration keyed by its module name (the gate supplies them, and the rule judges only those); path is the filesystem path of module's .hd, stamped into each diagnostic's source_location. HC-REF006 and HC-REF007 are separate codes because they call for different repairs: HC-REF007's finding is a name that is right and a shape that is wrong, and correcting it means either giving that module a door or moving the function to something that is a library by design — reporting it as one finding with HC-REF006 would hand a reader a repair that fits half the cases. It reads nothing and returns one Diagnostic per such seam at error severity (section 8); the empty list means every door a seam of this module named is a boundary_in of the module it named. It raises nothing."

  # ---- the cross-module seam orchestrator and door ----

  orchestrator fn run_seam_rules : (declarations: dict<str, Declaration>, paths: dict<str, str>, in_scope: list<str>) -> list<Diagnostic> invokes check_hc_ref006, check_hc_ref007 note "the orchestrator the seam door hands its inputs to (section 8, §HC-REF006/§HC-REF007). For each module named in in_scope — or every loaded module when in_scope is empty — it runs the two cross-module seam rules over that module's declaration and folds every finding into one list: for the module it calls check_hc_ref006 with the module's declaration, the whole declarations map and the module's .hd path, then check_hc_ref007 the same way, concatenating their diagnostics in that order across every in-scope module. Both rules in turn call declared_seams to derive that module's seams once, so the orchestrator never derives seams itself and never calls declared_seams directly. It resolves each in-scope module's seams against the COMPLETE declarations map, not against the in-scope subset, because a seam one in-scope module wrote may enter a module outside the scope and must still resolve to a door; reporting only the in-scope modules while resolving against all of them is what keeps the gate's answer independent of how it was invoked, so a commit that stages one module gets the same verdict on that module as a full sweep (§639). declarations is every loaded module's parsed .hd declaration keyed by its module name, a dict<str, Declaration>; the Declaration values are honest-design's IR, foreign here, which honest-check reads and never constructs. paths is each module's .hd filesystem path keyed by the same module name, passed through to each rule so a diagnostic names the file that wrote the offending seam. in_scope is the list of module names to report on — the gate's affected set — and the empty list means every loaded module is in scope. It reads nothing, opens no file and touches no world: the declarations arrive already parsed from the door. It returns the flat combined list of every Diagnostic the two rules produced across the in-scope modules, empty when every in-scope module's seams resolve. It raises nothing; an unresolved seam is a Diagnostic, never an exception."

  # check_seams is the door the honest-pre-commit gate reaches. The gate loads every framework module's .hd
  # declaration (it needs them for its other checks too), then calls this one function and gets back
  # the combined cross-module findings. It hands its inputs to one orchestrator (run_seam_rules) and
  # returns that orchestrator's diagnostics — thin, I/O only, no resolution logic. The declarations
  # arrive already parsed, so this path reads no file; read_declaration stays the filesystem reader
  # for the single-declaration --declaration crossing path, not this one.
  boundary_in fn check_seams : (declarations: dict<str, Declaration>, paths: dict<str, str>, in_scope: list<str>) -> list<Diagnostic> invokes run_seam_rules note "the door the honest-pre-commit dogfood gate calls to run honest-check's cross-module seam checks (section 8, §HC-REF006/§HC-REF007). It is the single entry point the external gate reaches into these checks: the gate parses every framework module's .hd declaration through honest-design's read_hd — it needs every declaration in view because a seam one module wrote may enter any other, and it holds them for its other checks too — then calls this one function to get back every unresolved cross-module seam as diagnostics. It carries only I/O: it hands its three inputs straight to the orchestrator run_seam_rules and returns the combined diagnostic list the orchestrator produced, holding no resolution logic itself, which lives on run_seam_rules's path through the pure rule functions. declarations is every loaded module's parsed declaration keyed by its module name, the dict<str, Declaration> the gate built by parsing each honest-*/honest-*.hd; the whole map must be in view because resolving an in-scope module's seam into module M needs M's declaration, whether or not M is in scope. The Declaration values are honest-design's IR for one `module` block, foreign to honest-check, which reads them and never constructs them. paths is each module's .hd filesystem path keyed by the same module name, so each diagnostic is stamped with the file that wrote the offending seam. in_scope is the list of module names to report on — the affected set the gate passes (the modules whose .hd or source a commit staged) — and it is the empty list when the whole framework is swept, which means every loaded module is in scope. The checks judge only the in-scope modules but resolve their seams against every declaration, so the gate's verdict on a module never changes with how the gate was invoked (§639). It returns the flat list of every Diagnostic the cross-module rules produced over the in-scope modules — one HC-REF006 error per seam into a module that declares no function of the named door, one HC-REF007 error per seam onto a function that is not a boundary_in — and the empty list when every in-scope module's seams resolve. It reads no world and writes none: because the gate supplies the declarations already parsed, this door opens no file. It raises nothing; an unresolved seam comes back as a Diagnostic, never as an exception."

  # The door the honest-pre-commit gate imports from honest_check.integration_rules. The gate calls this one
  # boundary_in, which leads inward to the orchestrator and then the pure rule functions, so the world
  # lands on a door and never on a pure function. A library import entry, which the entry-check reports
  # UNCHECKED (it confirms only the import, never the call) rather than flagging the door as an orphan.
  entry "import honest_check.integration_rules" -> check_seams

  # ======================================================================================
  # The shared extraction API (section 3.5). honest-check recognizes chains, state machines,
  # import aliases, template class references and watchlist membership to run its own rules;
  # honest-test needs the same recognitions and must not re-derive them, or a chain honest-check
  # checks and a chain honest-test enumerates could drift apart silently (section 3.5). So the
  # recognition is written once here, the one source of truth for the section 3.4 declaration
  # graph and the section 3.3 alias resolution, and exposed as five pure functions honest-test
  # consumes by importing the submodule that holds each — honest_check.declgraph,
  # honest_check.templates, honest_check.watchlists — exactly as the cross-module seam functions
  # are reached by importing honest_check.integration_rules. The five are public but are NOT part
  # of the CLI/startup __all__ surface; each is named by an import entry below, because each is
  # imported and called by honest-test on its own (there is no door or orchestrator to invoke
  # them), so each needs its own entry to be reachable rather than an orphan. They open no file,
  # execute none of the code they read, and raise nothing: a construct that cannot be recognized
  # is simply absent from the returned list.
  # ======================================================================================

  # tree-sitter's parsed node, as honest-check receives it from honest-parse. honest-parse owns
  # the shape and declares it foreign "tree-sitter"; honest-check does not own it and does not
  # construct it — honest-test produces it by calling honest-parse's parse and hands it in — so
  # here it is foreign to honest-parse, exactly as Declaration is foreign to honest-design.
  type Node = foreign "tree-sitter" note "one parsed syntax-tree node, the value honest-parse's parse produces over one source file (section 3.3, section 3.5). It is foreign because honest-parse, not honest-check, owns its shape: honest-test parses each file once by calling honest-parse's parse, then hands the resulting root node to resolve_aliases, extract_chains and extract_state_machines, which read the framework's construction call sites off it. honest-check receives the node and reads it, never constructs it — the same reason Declaration is foreign to honest-design. The same root node is passed to all three declgraph functions, because honest-test parses the file one time and reuses the one tree."

  # One chain a source file declares: the chain's declared name and the ordered names of its link
  # functions. extract_chains produces one per recognized chain; honest-test reads both fields.
  type ChainDecl = {
    name: str
    links: list<str>
  } note "one chain a source file declares, as recognized by the declaration-graph chain of section 3.4 and returned by extract_chains (section 3.5). name is the chain's declared name — the name bound to the chain() construction in the source. links is the ordered names of the chain's link functions, first link to last in the order they compose, the same adjacency HC002 crosses each pair's vocabularies over and HC-R002 reads. honest-test reads decl[\"name\"] to bind the chain to its live object through the imported module and decl[\"links\"] to enumerate the chain, so both fields carry exactly the names the source wrote. A chain extract_chains cannot recognize is absent from its list, never an error, so every ChainDecl names a chain the file really declares."

  # One state machine a source file declares: the name bound to the machine. extract_state_machines
  # produces one per recognized machine; honest-test reads the name.
  type MachineDecl = {
    name: str
  } note "one state machine a source file declares, as recognized by the state_machines of section 3.4 and returned by extract_state_machines (section 3.5) — the same construct the HC-SM rules (HC-SM01, HC-SM02, HC-SM05) check. name is the state machine's declared name, the name bound to the state_machine() construction in the source. honest-test reads decl[\"name\"] to bind the machine to its live object through the imported module and enumerate it. A machine extract_state_machines cannot recognize is absent from its list, never an error, so every MachineDecl names a machine the file really declares."

  # One static CSS class a template references. template_class_references produces one per static
  # class; honest-test reads reference["class"], so the field name is exactly `class`.
  type ClassRef = {
    class: str
  } note "one static CSS class a template references, as returned by template_class_references (section 3.5) — the same class recognition HC-REF003 resolves against the defined-class set (section 4.2). class is the class name as the template wrote it, one statically-known class token; honest-test reads reference[\"class\"], so the field name is exactly 'class'. A class value carrying interpolation (class=\"card {{ variant }}\") is dynamic and is skipped whole, exactly as HC-REF003 skips it, so every ClassRef names a fully static class the template really references. A template that references no static class yields the empty list, never an error."

  fn resolve_aliases : (root: Node, source: bytes) -> dict<str, str> note "the one import-alias resolver of section 3.3 and section 3.5, the single source of truth for the import-alias map both honest-check's own context builder and honest-test consume. It reads every import statement off the parsed tree and returns the map from each local name the source bound an honest-framework import to, to that import's canonical framework name, so an aliased import (from honest_type import vocabulary as v) is still recognized as the framework call it names. The returned map goes local_name -> canonical_name: a direct alias (vocabulary as v) maps v -> vocabulary, and a module alias (import honest_type as ht) maps ht.* -> honest_type.* so a dotted call through the alias resolves (section 3.3). It has two callers, and that is why it is a plain pure fn and not an edge. build_context calls it from inside the module, on the parsed tree and the source bytes it already holds, to fill the CheckContext's aliases field — the corrected section 3.4 wiring: build_context reuses this one resolver rather than deriving the map a second way, and a function the core calls is reached from inside, which an edge (an import-only surface, reached by nothing inside) is not. honest-test also reaches it by import (the entry below, honest_check.declgraph), parsing each file once and handing in the same two arguments, then passing the resulting map to extract_chains and extract_state_machines so the alias resolution the two extractors rely on is derived one time (section 3.5). root is the parsed syntax-tree node — honest-parse's parse over the file, the tree build_context parsed or the tree honest-test produced. source is that file's full source bytes, the same bytes honest-parse parsed — code in a language honest-parse recognizes (Python, JavaScript or TypeScript, Ruby, Go — section 3.2), never a .hd declaration — which resolve_aliases reads to recover each import statement's text. It opens no file, executes none of the code it reads, and returns the empty map when the source imports nothing from the framework. It raises nothing."

  edge fn extract_chains : (root: Node, source: bytes, aliases: dict<str, str>) -> list<ChainDecl> note "the chain recognizer of the section 3.4 declaration graph, exposed for honest-test (section 3.5). It finds every chain the source declares — each chain() construction, recognized through the aliases map so an aliased chain import still counts — and returns one ChainDecl per chain, carrying the chain's declared name and the ordered names of its link functions. This is the same chain construct HC002 crosses adjacent links' vocabularies over and HC-R002 reads, recognized once here so honest-check and honest-test never derive 'what is a chain' independently and drift silently (section 3.5). root is the parsed tree honest-test produced with honest-parse's parse. source is the file's full source bytes honest-parse parsed, code in a language honest-parse recognizes and never a .hd. aliases is the local-name-to-canonical-name map resolve_aliases returned for this same file, so an aliased chain import is recognized. honest-test binds each returned name to a live object through the imported module and enumerates the chain exhaustively. It opens no file, executes none of the code it reads, and returns the empty list when the file declares no chain; a construct it cannot recognize is absent from the list, never an error. It raises nothing."

  edge fn extract_state_machines : (root: Node, source: bytes, aliases: dict<str, str>) -> list<MachineDecl> note "the state-machine recognizer of the section 3.4 declaration graph, exposed for honest-test (section 3.5). It finds every state machine the source declares — each state_machine() construction, recognized through the aliases map so an aliased import still counts — and returns one MachineDecl per machine, carrying the machine's declared name. This is the same state_machines construct the HC-SM rules (HC-SM01, HC-SM02, HC-SM05) check, recognized once here so honest-check and honest-test never derive it independently and drift silently (section 3.5). root is the parsed tree honest-test produced with honest-parse's parse. source is the file's full source bytes honest-parse parsed, code in a language honest-parse recognizes and never a .hd. aliases is the local-name-to-canonical-name map resolve_aliases returned for this same file. honest-test binds each returned name to a live object through the imported module and enumerates the machine exhaustively. It opens no file, executes none of the code it reads, and returns the empty list when the file declares no state machine; a construct it cannot recognize is absent from the list, never an error. It raises nothing."

  edge fn template_class_references : (template: bytes) -> list<ClassRef> note "the template class-reference recognizer of HC-REF003, exposed for honest-test (section 3.5). It reads a component template's bytes and returns one ClassRef per static CSS class the template references, so honest-test can prove two components do not share a CSS namespace (honest-test section 7.1) against exactly the classes HC-REF003 resolves (section 4.2). template is the template's full markup as bytes — one component template, the form honest-parse's parse takes and honest-test holds a component template in, not a parsed node; it is parsed under the html grammar here, since the class references are read from the template itself. Each returned ClassRef carries one statically-known class token; a class value carrying interpolation (class=\"card {{ variant }}\") is dynamic and is skipped whole, exactly as HC-REF003 skips it (section 4.2), so every ClassRef names a fully static class the template really references. It opens no file, executes nothing, and returns the empty list when the template references no static class. It raises nothing."

  edge fn matches_watchlist : (name: str, watchlist: list<str>) -> bool note "the watchlist membership test HC008 runs, exposed for honest-test (section 3.5) so honest-test checks its own non-determinism watchlist against honest-check's and the two tools trap exactly the same calls (honest-test section 4.5). It answers whether a dotted call name matches an entry in a watchlist: an entry that carries no '*' matches by exact equality, and an entry that carries a '*' matches as a glob, the same membership HC008 runs against its IO_WATCH_LIST and NONDETERMINISTIC_WATCH_LIST (section 4.2). name is the dotted call name read off a call site — for example 'time.time', 'os.environ.get', 'requests.get' — the name HC008 tests. watchlist is the list of watchlist entries to test against, each a dotted name, some carrying a '*' glob (for example 'random.*', 'subprocess.run', 'fs.*'), exactly the entries section 4.2's IO_WATCH_LIST and NONDETERMINISTIC_WATCH_LIST hold for a language. It returns true when name matches any entry and false when it matches none. It reads nothing, opens nothing, executes nothing, and raises nothing."

  # Each of the five shared-extraction functions is imported and called by honest-test on its own,
  # so each is named by its own import entry — the seam pattern, one entry per imported public
  # function. The entry-check confirms only the import and reports each UNCHECKED, which is what
  # keeps a library function reached only from another module out of the orphan reds.
  entry "import honest_check.declgraph" -> resolve_aliases
  entry "import honest_check.declgraph" -> extract_chains
  entry "import honest_check.declgraph" -> extract_state_machines
  entry "import honest_check.templates" -> template_class_references
  entry "import honest_check.watchlists" -> matches_watchlist

  # The per-file check context: the one parse a run shares across the helper layer and the
  # rule sub-cores (section 3.4, section 3.5). build_context builds it once and the orchestrator
  # threads it in; it is the one type build_context produces (section 3.4).
  type CheckContext = {
    root: Node
    nodes: list<Node>
    aliases: dict<str, str>
    roles: dict<str, str>
    source: bytes
    path: str
    rule_config: RuleConfig
  } note "the shared parse context build_context builds once per source and the orchestrator threads through check_source, the two rule sub-cores and the AST helper layer (nodes, analysis, bindings). It is the one record build_context produces and the one type the whole helper layer takes (section 3.4): no function but build_context builds it, and every function that reads it receives it from the caller above. root is the parsed tree honest-parse produced over the source, nodes its pre-walked node list, aliases the honest_type import-alias map build_context resolved (section 3.3), roles the declared function-to-role map, source the raw bytes of the file under check, path the filesystem path that source came from (under whose extension honest-parse selected the grammar), rule_config the run's resolved per-rule settings. check_source and the rule sub-cores read this context rather than parsing anything themselves, which is why only build_context parses and the rest stay pure of I/O and parsing."

  # The one builder of the check context and the only producer of CheckContext (section 3.4).
  # An orchestrator (check_paths at startup, run_check for the CLI) reads the file and the config
  # through the thin readers, then hands build_context the source, the path and the configuration;
  # build_context turns them into the one shared parse, and passes it back for the orchestrator to
  # thread into check_source. Declared because the spec names it: "The design declares build_context
  # as a pure function ... Only build_context builds it" (section 3.4).
  fn build_context : (source: bytes, path: str, rule_config: RuleConfig) -> CheckContext invokes resolve_aliases note "the pure context builder (section 3.4), the one function that assembles the per-source CheckContext and the only producer of that type anywhere in the module. An orchestrator — check_paths at startup, run_check for the CLI — already read the file's text with read_source and the project's rule settings with load_config, and hands build_context those three values; build_context parses the source under the grammar honest-parse selects from the path, resolves the honest_type import aliases, walks the syntax-tree nodes into the node list, reads the declared function-to-role map, and returns one CheckContext holding the parsed root, the node list, the import-alias map, the role map, the source bytes, the path and the rule configuration. It runs no rule and does no input or output: the orchestrator did the reading, and build_context only turns the already-read text into the shared parse the rule sub-cores read, so the context has one builder and a clear line of callers (section 3.4). source is the full text of one honest-framework application source file as bytes — code in a language honest-parse recognizes (Python, JavaScript or TypeScript, Ruby, Go — section 3.2, section 4.1), never a .hd declaration and never honest-check's own internals — the text read_source pulled from disk and the orchestrator passed down. path is the filesystem path that text came from; honest-parse owns the extension-to-grammar table (section 3.2), and build_context detects the grammar to parse this source under from path's extension and stamps the path into the context so each diagnostic's source_location names the file. It opens nothing: path is used to pick the grammar, not to re-read the file, which read_source already did. rule_config is the run's resolved per-rule settings, a RuleConfig load_config produced at the boundary, carried into the context so the one configurable rule runs under the project's setting; honest-check's single configurable rule is HC-OR003, whose min_run has no built-in default, so an empty map means HC-OR003 does not fire (§HC-OR003). It invokes resolve_aliases, not import_aliases, to resolve the section 3.3 import-alias map it stores in the returned context — import_aliases reads the alias map off a finished CheckContext, and the context does not exist yet while build_context is assembling it, so build_context calls resolve_aliases on the parsed tree and the source bytes it already holds. resolve_aliases is the one alias resolver of section 3.3 and section 3.5, so build_context reuses it rather than deriving the map a second way; the resulting alias map is the alias resolution the construction rules rely on to recognize a framework constructor called under an alias. build_context performs the parse and the node walk itself. It returns the one CheckContext; check_source and the rule sub-cores receive it from the orchestrator and never build their own, so build_context is the sole place a context is constructed (section 3.4). It raises nothing."
  fn diagnostic : (ctx: CheckContext, rule_id: str, severity: str, message: str, node: Node) -> Diagnostic note "diagnostic: a helper of the split check_source rule engine (section 3.4)."
  fn literal_string : (node: Node) -> str note "The Python string a `string` node spells, quotes stripped."
  fn string_members : (ctx: CheckContext, node: Node) -> list<str> note "The string values a set/list/tuple node holds, in source order."
  fn call_arguments : (ctx: CheckContext, call_node: Node) -> any note "The (positional nodes, keyword name -> value node) of a call."
  fn canonical_name : (ctx: CheckContext, call_node: Node) -> str | NOTHING note "The constructor name a call resolves to, or None when the call is not a framework constructor. A direct constructor-name call counts, and an aliased one resolves through the import (section 3.3)."
  fn base_types_of : (ctx: CheckContext, dictionary_node: Node) -> any note "The base-type map of a vocabulary's first argument: name -> (kind, payload). kind is 'set' with a frozenset of members, or 'predicate' with the recognizer's value node (a lambda or any other opaque recognizer honest-check cannot run). HC009"
  fn predicate_is_risky : (value: Node) -> bool note "True when a predicate's expression holds a node that throws on a non-matching input (§HC009): a call to bare `int` or `float`, a subscript access, or a `/` or `//` division. A predicate that throws is a recognizer that rejects by exception "
  fn enclosing_function : (node: Node) -> Node | NOTHING note "The nearest enclosing function_definition of a node, climbing parents, or None at module scope. A node's nearest enclosing function is the scope that owns it, so a nonlocal or a mutation reads as belonging to the function it sits directly i"
  fn underscore_self_name : (target: Node) -> str | NOTHING note "The attribute name of a `self._name` assignment target whose name starts with an underscore, or None when the target is not that shape (§HC-P007)."
  fn called_name : (ctx: CheckContext, call_node: Node) -> str | NOTHING note "The name a call invokes — a bare identifier, or the final attribute of a dotted call such as `window.addEventListener` — or None (§HC-P011)."
  fn own_body : (fdef: Node) -> any note "The body descendants belonging to a function itself, not descending into a deeper nested def, lambda or decorated def — those statements are the inner scope's own (§4.2). A `nonlocal` and a mutation count only where they sit directly in thi"
  fn nonlocal_names : (statements: list<Node>) -> any note "The names declared by the `nonlocal` statements directly in a function body."
  fn decorators_of : (ctx: CheckContext, fdef: Node) -> any note "The decorator nodes applied to a function — the `decorator` children of the enclosing `decorated_definition`, or an empty list for an undecorated function. The boundary signal HC-P002, HC-P004 and HC-P005 read, and the cache signal HC-P006 "
  fn decorator_expression : (decorator: Node) -> Node note "The expression a decorator applies — the identifier, attribute or call after the `@`. A parsed decorator always carries that expression as its first named child."
  fn decorator_target : (decorator: Node) -> str | NOTHING note "The final name a decorator names — `boundary` from `@boundary`, `lru_cache` from `@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=1)` — or None when it is neither a plain nor a dotted name, called or bare (§4.2, §5.6)."
  fn is_boundary_decorator : (ctx: CheckContext, decorator: Node) -> bool note "True when a decorator declares its function a boundary — `@boundary` (bare or called) or `@link(boundary=True)` (§4.2)."
  fn in_non_boundary_function : (ctx: CheckContext, node: Node) -> bool note "True when a node's nearest enclosing function exists, carries no boundary decorator, and the declaration gives its name no door role — the scope HC-P002, HC-P004 and HC-P005 fire in (§4.2, §5.4, §5.5). A node at module scope, or in a bounda"
  fn is_immutable_value : (value: Node) -> bool note "True when a module-level binding's right side is provably immutable (§5.4, §872): a number, a string, a bool, None, a tuple literal, a negated immutable, or a `frozenset(...)` call. Everything else — a list, dict or set literal, a comprehen"
  fn module_mutable_globals : (ctx: CheckContext) -> any note "The module-level names bound to a mutable (or not provably immutable) value — the globals a read of which makes a link impure (§5.4, §872). A name bound to an immutable value, and an imported or def/class name (never an assignment target), "
  fn param_names : (param: Node) -> any note "The names a single parameter node binds — a bare identifier, or the `name` of a typed or defaulted parameter. A splat or other shape binds nothing here."
  fn link_locals : (fdef: Node, body: list<Node>) -> any note "The names local to a link — its parameters plus the names it assigns in its own body. A read of one of these is not a read of a module global (§5.4)."
  fn is_attribute_suffix : (node: Node) -> bool note "True when an identifier is the attribute part of an `x.name` access — the `.name`, not a read of `name` itself. Reading `config.timeout` reads `config`, not a global called `timeout` (§5.4)."
  fn is_keyword_name : (node: Node) -> bool note "True when an identifier is a keyword argument's name — the `k` in `f(k=v)`, which names a parameter of the callee, not a global read (§5.4)."
  fn watched : (target: Node, watch: set<str>) -> bool note "True when a call's dotted target is on a watch list: an exact entry matches by equality, and an entry carrying a `*` matches as a glob — a trailing `.*` names any attribute of a module, a trailing `*` is a prefix, a `*` between dots is one "
  fn dispatch_variable : (ctx: CheckContext, condition: Node) -> str | NOTHING note "The variable name an `if`/`elif` condition dispatches on — the left identifier of an `identifier == <value>` equality test — or None when the condition is not that shape (§HC-P001)."
  fn within_class_init : (ctx: CheckContext, node: Node) -> any note "True when a node's nearest enclosing function is an `__init__` that is a method of a class — the scope HC-P007 reads for hidden instance state (§4.2)."
  fn mutated_names : (ctx: CheckContext, statements: list<Node>) -> any note "The names an assignment or augmented assignment targets directly in a function body — the left of each `x = ...` or `x += ...` whose target is a plain identifier."
  fn is_empty_default : (ctx: CheckContext, value: Node) -> bool note "True when a parameter's default stands for absence rather than a baked-in value (§HC-P015): None, False, a zero number, an empty string, an empty list/dict/tuple literal, or an empty-constructor call. A populated literal, a non-zero number,"
  fn dotted_target : (ctx: CheckContext, call_node: Node) -> any note "The dotted name a call invokes — `open`, `os.system`, `requests.get`, `urllib.request.urlopen` — or None when the target is not a plain or dotted name, such as a call on a call's result (§5.4)."
  fn link_is_impure : (ctx: CheckContext, fdef: Node, watch: set<str>, mutable_globals: set<str>) -> any note "True when a link's own body calls a watched function or reads a module-level mutable global (§HC008). The body search does not descend into a nested def or lambda, whose statements are that inner scope's own."
  fn is_link_decorated : (ctx: CheckContext, fdef: Node) -> bool note "True when a function carries a `@link` decorator, bare or called — the chain-link declaration HC008 and HC001 both read for a declared link (§HC008, §4.2)."
  fn local_function_links : (ctx: CheckContext) -> any note "Each function defined in this file, mapped to whether it carries a `@link` decorator (§4.2, HC001). A chain positional that resolves to one of these names is a local link declaration honest-check can read; a name not here is defined elsewhe"
  fn is_orchestrator : (ctx: CheckContext, fdef: Node) -> bool note "True when a function carries an `@orchestrator` decorator, bare or called — matched by decorator name the same way a boundary decorator is (§4.2, HC-OR001)."
  fn longest_shared_run : (ctx: CheckContext, calls_a: list<str>, calls_b: list<str>) -> any note "The length of the longest run of consecutive equivalent call names two call sequences share (§HC-OR003). A contiguous common substring over the two lists, found by the standard row-by-row dynamic program; 0 when they share no adjacent run."
  fn link_declares_emits : (ctx: CheckContext, fdef: Node) -> any note "True when a function carries a `@link` decorator that passes an `emits` keyword — the emits vocabulary HC-P017 requires of an HTTP-producing link (§HC-P017). Read the same way is_boundary_decorator reads `boundary`: from the decorator call'"
  fn construction_maps : (ctx: CheckContext) -> any note "Each file-local variable bound to a `vocabulary(...)` or a `binding(...)` call, mapped to that construction call node (§HC004, §HC005). A name bound to anything else is not here; a name honest-check cannot resolve to one of these two constr"
  fn features_vocabulary : (ctx: CheckContext) -> any note "The feature-flag vocabulary this module declares: each flag name mapped to the set of states its `FEATURES[flag][\"states\"]` set names (§7, HC-HF001/HC-HF002). Read only from a module-scope `FEATURES = {...}` dict literal — the same top-leve"
  fn feature_state_flag : (ctx: CheckContext, call_node: Node) -> str | NOTHING note "The flag a `feature_state(state, \"flag\")` call names — its second positional, a string literal — or None when the call names fewer than two positionals or a dynamic (non-literal) flag (§7). The call is resolved by name through called_name."
  fn module_dict_literals : (ctx: CheckContext) -> any note "Each module-scope name bound to a dict literal, mapped to the set of string keys the literal declares (§7, HC-HF002). A name bound to anything but a module-scope dict literal is not here, so a handler table that resolves to no such name is "
  fn vocabulary_types : (ctx: CheckContext, vocab_call: Node) -> any note "The type surface of a `vocabulary(...)` call: its base-type names, its composed-type names, the base names its composed types require, and the base names its composed types capture (§HC004, §HC005). base_types_of reads the first-argument di"
  fn binding_keys : (ctx: CheckContext, binding_call: Node) -> any note "The type names a `binding(...)` call binds — the keys of its first-argument table (§HC004, §HC005)."
  fn vocabulary_binding_pairs : (ctx: CheckContext) -> any note "Each (vocabulary calls, binding call) a classify or link pairs by name (§HC004, §HC005). A `classify(tokens, vocab, bind=b)` pairs positional 2 with the `bind` keyword, and its vocabulary list is the one vocab. A `link(accepts=v, emits=e, b"
  fn check_construction : (ctx: CheckContext) -> list<Diagnostic> invokes canonical_name, call_arguments, string_members, literal_string, base_types_of, vocabulary_types, binding_keys, construction_maps, vocabulary_binding_pairs, diagnostic note "the construction-time rule sub-core (section 4.1), one of the two rule sub-cores check_source threads the CheckContext through. It runs the construction-time rules — HC001, HC003, HC006, HC007, HC011 and the HC-SM rules (HC-SM01, HC-SM02, HC-SM05) — dispatched by the framework constructor each call site resolves to, and reaches the shared vocabulary-and-binding helper layer these rules read: canonical_name resolves a call site to the constructor it names (vocabulary, binding, chain, composed or state_machine) through the import aliases; call_arguments, string_members and literal_string read that call's positional and keyword arguments and the string members of its set and sequence literals; base_types_of and vocabulary_types read a vocabulary's base-type map and its composed-type surface (the base names its composed types require and capture) that HC003 and HC006 cross; binding_keys reads a binding's bound type names, and construction_maps and vocabulary_binding_pairs pair each vocabulary construction with its binding so the dead-type and unused-binding checks can judge them; diagnostic builds each finding. The per-rule logic nests here as data; ctx carries the one parse. It returns the construction-time findings as data and raises nothing. The spec gives the layer (section 3.4: the rules call the shared helper layer) but not the exact per-helper call edge; the helpers listed here are the construction-reading helpers whose own notes cite the construction and vocabulary rules this sub-core runs (section 4.1)."
  fn check_per_file : (ctx: CheckContext) -> list<Diagnostic> invokes enclosing_function, own_body, in_non_boundary_function, decorators_of, decorator_expression, decorator_target, is_boundary_decorator, underscore_self_name, within_class_init, called_name, dispatch_variable, is_immutable_value, module_mutable_globals, param_names, link_locals, is_attribute_suffix, is_keyword_name, mutated_names, nonlocal_names, dotted_target, watched, link_is_impure, is_link_decorated, local_function_links, link_declares_emits, is_orchestrator, longest_shared_run, predicate_is_risky, is_empty_default, features_vocabulary, feature_state_flag, module_dict_literals, diagnostic note "the static per-file rule sub-core (section 4.2), the second rule sub-core check_source threads the CheckContext through. It runs the per-file rules — the HC-P purity rules, the HC chain/link rules (HC009, HC010 and the vocabulary rules HC004/HC005) and the HC-HF feature-flag rules — folded over the declaration graph, and reaches the shared AST helper layer the rules read: the scope helpers enclosing_function, own_body and in_non_boundary_function locate a node's owning function and decide whether it is a non-boundary scope (HC-P002, HC-P004, HC-P005); the decorator helpers decorators_of, decorator_expression, decorator_target, is_boundary_decorator, is_link_decorated, is_orchestrator and link_declares_emits read a function's role, link and emission declarations; underscore_self_name and within_class_init find hidden instance state (HC-P007); called_name and dispatch_variable read a call target and an if/elif dispatch discriminant (HC-P011, HC-P001); is_immutable_value, module_mutable_globals, param_names, link_locals, is_attribute_suffix, is_keyword_name, mutated_names, nonlocal_names, dotted_target, watched and link_is_impure decide link impurity and mutable-closure state (HC008, HC-P016); local_function_links resolves a chain positional to a local link declaration (HC001); longest_shared_run measures orchestrator duplication (HC-OR003); predicate_is_risky flags a throwing predicate (HC009); is_empty_default flags an absence-standing default parameter (HC-P015); features_vocabulary, feature_state_flag and module_dict_literals read the feature-flag vocabulary and handler tables (HC-HF001, HC-HF002); diagnostic builds each finding. The per-rule logic nests here as data; ctx carries the one parse. It returns the per-file findings as data and raises nothing. The spec gives the layer (section 3.4: the rules call the shared helper layer) but not the exact per-helper call edge; the helpers listed here are the per-file helpers whose own notes cite the HC-P, chain/link and HC-HF rules this sub-core runs (section 4.2)."
  fn apply_suppression : (ctx: CheckContext, raw_diagnostics: list<Diagnostic>) -> list<Diagnostic> invokes diagnostic note "the suppression pass (section 7), the stage check_source runs over the combined findings of the two rule sub-cores. It reads the inline and file-level suppression directives off the parse in ctx and applies them to raw_diagnostics: a suppressed finding is downgraded to info rather than dropped, a directive that suppressed nothing is reported as HC-SUP001 (dead directive) and a directive carrying no reason as HC-SUP002, each of those two findings built with diagnostic. The directive parsers nest here as data; ctx carries the one parse and raw_diagnostics is the sub-cores' combined output. It returns the suppression-resolved diagnostics and raises nothing. diagnostic is the one shared helper this pass reaches, to build the HC-SUP001 and HC-SUP002 findings; the spec gives the layer (section 3.4) and section 7 the two suppression guarantees, and this is the only helper the pass needs."
